  'Option B (AWS IAM Identity Center) is designed for centrally managing access to multiple AWS accounts and business applications, providing a single sign-on experience for users. Option A (Amazon Cognito) is for user authentication and authorization for web and mobile apps. Option C (AWS KMS) is for managing encryption keys. Option D (AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store) is for managing configuration data and secrets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_58',
  'What is the primary purpose of an AWS IAM policy?',
  'mcq',
  '["To define the network configuration for a VPC","To specify permissions for an IAM identity or AWS resource","To launch an Amazon EC2 instance","To store objects in Amazon S3"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (To specify permissions for an IAM identity or AWS resource) is the primary purpose of an AWS IAM policy. Policies are JSON documents that explicitly define what actions are allowed or denied on which resources. Option A relates to Amazon VPC. Option C is an action performed by a user or service, not the policy itself. Option D describes the function of Amazon S3.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_59',
  'A security best practice for the AWS account root user is to:',
  'mcq',
  '["Use it for daily administrative tasks","Delete it after creating the first IAM user","Enable Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) and use it only for tasks that require root permissions","Share its credentials with all administrators for convenience"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Enable Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) and use it only for tasks that require root permissions) is the correct security best practice. The root user has unrestricted access and should be secured with MFA and used sparingly for only specific tasks. Option A (Using root account for daily operations) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.1-001) as it violates the principle of least privilege. Option B is incorrect as the root user cannot be deleted. Option D is a severe security risk and violates best practices.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_60',
  'What is the primary purpose of Service Control Policies (SCPs) in AWS Organizations?',
  'mcq',
  '["To define network routing for VPCs","To manage billing across multiple AWS accounts","To centrally control the maximum available permissions for all accounts in an organization","To automate the deployment of applications"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (To centrally control the maximum available permissions for all accounts in an organization) is the primary purpose of SCPs. They are a type of organization policy that you can use to manage permissions in your organization. SCPs offer central control over the maximum available permissions for all accounts in your organization. Option A relates to Amazon VPC. Option B is handled by AWS Organizations consolidated billing. Option D is handled by services like AWS CloudFormation or AWS CodeDeploy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_61',
  'A company needs to grant temporary, federated access to AWS resources for its employees who are already authenticated with an on-premises Active Directory. Which AWS service should be used to integrate with the Active Directory and provide single sign-on to AWS accounts?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS IAM Identity Center (successor to AWS Single Sign-On)","AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","Amazon Cognito User Pools","AWS Organizations"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS IAM Identity Center, which simplifies the management of access to multiple AWS accounts and business applications. It can be integrated with on-premises Active Directory to provide federated access and single sign-on for employees. Option B (incorrect) is AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM), which manages users, groups, roles, and permissions within a single AWS account. While IAM roles are used by IAM Identity Center, IAM itself does not provide the federated identity management capabilities for an entire organization from an external identity source like Active Directory. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon Cognito User Pools, which are primarily used for customer-facing applications to manage user sign-up, sign-in, and access control, not for enterprise employee federation. Option D (incorrect) is AWS Organizations, which helps you centrally manage and govern your environment as you grow and scale your AWS resources. While it''s used in multi-account strategies, it does not directly provide identity federation with Active Directory for user authentication.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_62',
  'Which principle should be applied when designing IAM policies to ensure that users and roles have only the necessary permissions to perform their tasks, thereby minimizing potential security risks?',
  'mcq',
  '["Principle of least privilege","Principle of shared responsibility","Principle of defense in depth","Principle of operational excellence"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the Principle of least privilege, which dictates that users and roles should be granted only the minimum permissions required to perform their specific job functions. This is a fundamental security best practice to reduce the attack surface. Option B (incorrect) is the Principle of shared responsibility, which defines the security responsibilities between AWS and the customer, not a principle for granting permissions. Option C (incorrect) is the Principle of defense in depth, which involves layering multiple security controls to protect resources, but it doesn''t specifically define how to grant individual permissions. Option D (incorrect) is the Principle of operational excellence, which is one of the pillars of the AWS Well-Architected Framework, focusing on running and monitoring systems effectively, but it''s not a principle for access control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_63',
  'A Solutions Architect needs to prevent the AWS root user account from being used for daily administrative tasks. What is the recommended best practice for securing the root user account?',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) on the root user and use an IAM user for daily tasks.","Delete the root user access keys and rely solely on IAM users.","Create an IAM role for the root user and assign it to administrators.","Restrict root user access by applying an explicit deny policy to the root user."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the recommended best practice. The root user account has unrestricted access to all AWS services and resources. It should be secured with a strong password and Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA), and then rarely used. Daily administrative tasks should be performed by IAM users or roles with specific, limited permissions. Option B (incorrect) is not feasible as the root user does not have access keys by default for programmatic access; it uses email and password. Even if it did, deleting them wouldn''t prevent console login. Option C (incorrect) is not possible. You cannot create an IAM role for the root user; the root user is a distinct entity. IAM roles are assumed by IAM users or AWS services. Option D (incorrect) is not effective. The root user has ultimate permissions and cannot have its own permissions restricted by IAM policies, as it bypasses IAM policies applied to itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_64',
  'An application running on an Amazon EC2 instance needs to securely access objects in an Amazon S3 bucket. What is the MOST secure and operationally efficient method to grant the EC2 instance these permissions?',
  'mcq',
  '["Attach an IAM role to the EC2 instance with the necessary S3 permissions.","Generate IAM user access keys and embed them in the application code on the EC2 instance.","Store S3 access credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store and retrieve them from the application.","Create a resource-based policy on the S3 bucket that explicitly allows the EC2 instance''s public IP address."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most secure and operationally efficient method. Attaching an IAM role to an EC2 instance allows the instance to receive temporary credentials automatically, eliminating the need to hardcode or manually manage credentials. This aligns with the principle of least privilege and reduces the risk of credential compromise. Option B (incorrect) represents an anti-pattern ([ap-1.1-002]). Embedding long-term access keys in application code is highly insecure, difficult to rotate, and poses a significant risk if the code is exposed. Option C (incorrect) is a valid method for storing secrets, but for EC2 instances needing AWS service access, IAM roles are preferred because they provide temporary, auto-rotated credentials without the application needing to explicitly retrieve them, reducing operational overhead. Option D (incorrect) is less secure and less flexible. Relying on an EC2 instance''s public IP address is problematic because public IPs can change, and it doesn''t provide granular IAM control over the instance''s identity, making it harder to manage and audit.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_65',
  'Which AWS service is used to manage access to AWS accounts and applications for workforce users, often integrating with existing corporate directories?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS IAM Identity Center","Amazon Cognito","AWS Organizations","AWS Single Sign-On"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS IAM Identity Center (successor to AWS Single Sign-On). It is designed to centrally manage access to multiple AWS accounts and business applications for workforce users, and it integrates with various identity sources, including corporate directories. Option B (incorrect) is Amazon Cognito, which is primarily for customer-facing applications to manage user authentication and authorization. Option C (incorrect) is AWS Organizations, which helps manage multiple AWS accounts but does not directly handle user authentication and authorization for workforce users. Option D (incorrect) is AWS Single Sign-On, which is the previous name for AWS IAM Identity Center. While technically correct in functionality, the current terminology is AWS IAM Identity Center.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_66',
  'A security team requires a centralized way to apply preventative security controls across multiple AWS accounts in an organization. Which AWS service and feature combination can achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Organizations with Service Control Policies (SCPs)","AWS IAM Identity Center with permission sets","Amazon GuardDuty with custom detection rules","AWS Config with conformance packs"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS Organizations with Service Control Policies (SCPs). SCPs allow you to set maximum permissions for IAM users and roles in all member accounts of an organization, providing a centralized way to enforce preventative security controls across multiple accounts. Option B (incorrect) is AWS IAM Identity Center with permission sets, which manages workforce user access to accounts but does not apply preventative controls at the organizational level like SCPs. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon GuardDuty with custom detection rules, which is a threat detection service, not a preventative control mechanism for access. Option D (incorrect) is AWS Config with conformance packs, which is used for auditing and enforcing compliance of resource configurations, but it''s a detective control, not a preventative access control like SCPs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_67',
  'Which AWS global infrastructure component is composed of one or more discrete data centers, each with redundant power, networking, and connectivity, and is isolated from failures in other locations?',
  'mcq',
  '["Availability Zone","AWS Region","Edge Location","Local Zone"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is an Availability Zone. An Availability Zone (AZ) is one or more discrete data centers with redundant power, networking, and connectivity, designed to be isolated from failures in other Availability Zones. This provides high availability and fault tolerance within an AWS Region. Option B (incorrect) is an AWS Region, which is a geographical area that contains multiple, isolated Availability Zones. Option C (incorrect) is an Edge Location, which is part of the global network for services like Amazon CloudFront and AWS Global Accelerator, used for content delivery and caching, not for hosting core compute resources in the same way an AZ does. Option D (incorrect) is a Local Zone, which extends an AWS Region into a geographic area closer to users, but an Availability Zone is the fundamental building block for fault-tolerant architecture within a Region.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_68',
  'What is the primary purpose of AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)?',
  'mcq',
  '["To securely control access to AWS services and resources","To provide a global content delivery network","To monitor and log API calls across AWS services","To manage domain names and DNS records"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the primary purpose of AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM). IAM enables you to securely control who is authenticated (signed in) and authorized (has permissions) to use AWS resources. Option B (incorrect) describes Amazon CloudFront, which is a content delivery network. Option C (incorrect) describes AWS CloudTrail, which logs API calls. Option D (incorrect) describes Amazon Route 53, which is a domain name system web service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_69',
  'A large enterprise with multiple AWS accounts for different departments needs to centralize identity management and provide single sign-on (SSO) access to various AWS accounts and business applications. The security team mandates that user identities must be managed from an existing on-premises Active Directory, and access should be granted based on group membership. The solution must minimize administrative overhead for managing user permissions across accounts. Which combination of AWS services should a Solutions Architect recommend to meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure AWS IAM Identity Center (successor to AWS SSO) and integrate it with AWS Directory Service for Microsoft Active Directory, then assign permissions using permission sets.","Manually create IAM users in each AWS account, then use IAM roles for cross-account access, and synchronize passwords with the on-premises Active Directory.","Set up an Amazon Cognito User Pool to manage user identities, then use an Amazon Cognito Identity Pool to federate access to AWS resources across accounts.","Deploy a custom identity broker on an Amazon EC2 instance within a central account, integrating it with the on-premises Active Directory and distributing temporary credentials."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most appropriate solution. AWS IAM Identity Center (successor to AWS SSO) is designed for centralizing access to multiple AWS accounts and business applications. It can be integrated with AWS Directory Service for Microsoft Active Directory, which in turn can be connected to an on-premises Active Directory. Permission sets in IAM Identity Center allow for defining permissions once and applying them across multiple accounts, significantly reducing administrative overhead and enabling group-based access. Option B is incorrect because manually creating IAM users in each account and managing cross-account roles is highly complex and increases administrative overhead, especially for a large enterprise with many accounts and users. Password synchronization is also not a robust SSO solution. Option C is incorrect because Amazon Cognito User Pools are primarily for customer-facing web and mobile applications, not typically for enterprise workforce identity management and SSO to AWS accounts. While Identity Pools can provide temporary AWS credentials, Cognito is not designed for direct integration with on-premises Active Directory for enterprise SSO in this manner. This represents anti-pattern [ap-1.1-004] by confusing Cognito''s primary use case. Option D is incorrect because deploying a custom identity broker on Amazon EC2 introduces significant operational overhead for development, maintenance, scaling, and security of the custom solution, which contradicts the requirement for the least operational overhead.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_70',
  'A global financial institution is migrating a legacy on-premises application to AWS. The application processes highly sensitive customer data and requires strict adherence to the principle of least privilege. Developers need temporary credentials to interact with AWS services like Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB from their application code running on Amazon EC2 instances. The security team explicitly forbids embedding long-term AWS access keys directly into application code or configuration files due to the risk of exposure and difficulty in rotation. Which approach should a Solutions Architect recommend to securely provide credentials to the application?',
  'mcq',
  '["Create an IAM role with the necessary permissions and attach it to the Amazon EC2 instances running the application.","Generate IAM user access keys for each developer and distribute them securely, requiring manual rotation every 90 days.","Store IAM user access keys in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store as SecureString parameters and retrieve them at application startup.","Embed IAM user access keys directly into the application''s environment variables on the Amazon EC2 instances."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most secure and operationally efficient method. Attaching an IAM role to an Amazon EC2 instance allows the instance to automatically receive temporary credentials from the EC2 instance metadata service. These credentials are short-lived and automatically rotated by AWS, eliminating the need to embed long-term access keys in the application code and adhering to the principle of least privilege. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.1-002]. Option B is incorrect because generating and distributing individual IAM user access keys for developers, even with secure distribution, introduces significant management overhead for rotation and revocation, and still involves long-term credentials which are explicitly forbidden. Option C is incorrect because while AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store SecureString provides encrypted storage, retrieving and managing long-term access keys at application startup still means the application is handling long-term credentials, which is less secure than using temporary credentials provided by an IAM role. It also doesn''t fully eliminate the risk of exposure if the retrieval mechanism is compromised. Option D is incorrect because embedding IAM user access keys directly into environment variables is a severe security risk, as these can be easily accessed and exposed, violating the security team''s mandate and representing anti-pattern [ap-1.1-002].',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_71',
  'A company is designing a multi-account AWS environment using AWS Organizations. The security team requires that all accounts, except for a designated ''security audit'' account, be prevented from creating or modifying IAM roles that allow access from external AWS accounts. This policy must be enforced at the organizational level to ensure compliance and prevent unauthorized cross-account access, even if an administrator in a member account attempts to override it. Which AWS Organizations feature should a Solutions Architect use to implement this control?',
  'mcq',
  '["Service Control Policies (SCPs) applied to the organizational unit (OU) containing all member accounts, excluding the security audit account.","IAM policies attached to the IAM roles within each member account, explicitly denying cross-account role creation.","AWS Config rules deployed in each member account to detect and remediate non-compliant IAM role configurations.","AWS CloudTrail logs monitored by Amazon GuardDuty to alert on any attempts to create unauthorized cross-account roles."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective solution for enforcing this control at the organizational level. Service Control Policies (SCPs) in AWS Organizations allow you to centrally manage permissions for all accounts in your organization. An SCP can explicitly deny the creation or modification of IAM roles that allow access from external accounts, and this policy cannot be overridden by IAM policies within the member accounts. Applying it to an OU and excluding the security audit account ensures the necessary exception. Option B is incorrect because IAM policies attached to roles within member accounts can be modified or circumvented by administrators in those accounts, failing to provide the centralized, un-overrideable control required by the security team. Option C is incorrect because AWS Config rules are for detecting and optionally remediating non-compliant resources after they have been created, rather than preventing their creation in the first place, which is a proactive security requirement. Option D is incorrect because AWS CloudTrail and Amazon GuardDuty provide detection and alerting capabilities for suspicious activity, but they do not prevent the creation of unauthorized resources, which is the primary goal of the security team.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_72',
  'A large enterprise is implementing a multi-account strategy using AWS Organizations and Control Tower. The security team wants to enforce a baseline set of security controls across all member accounts to prevent common misconfigurations, such as disabling AWS CloudTrail or creating overly permissive S3 bucket policies. These controls must be preventative and apply to new and existing accounts without manual intervention in each account. Which AWS Organizations feature should a Solutions Architect leverage to meet these requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Service Control Policies (SCPs) applied at the root of the organization or to specific Organizational Units (OUs).","AWS Config rules deployed in each member account via a centralized AWS CloudFormation StackSet.","IAM policies attached to the IAM roles within each member account, managed by a central administrator.","Amazon GuardDuty enabled in all member accounts to detect and alert on suspicious activities related to misconfigurations."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective solution. Service Control Policies (SCPs) in AWS Organizations allow you to define the maximum available permissions for all accounts within an OU or the entire organization. SCPs are preventative controls that cannot be overridden by IAM policies in member accounts, making them ideal for enforcing baseline security controls like preventing CloudTrail disabling or overly permissive S3 policies across all accounts without manual intervention. Option B is incorrect because AWS Config rules are detective and often reactive controls, meaning they detect non-compliance after it occurs. While they can be deployed centrally via StackSets, they don''t prevent actions from happening in the first place, which is the requirement for preventative controls. Option C is incorrect because IAM policies are managed within each account and can be modified or circumvented by administrators in those accounts, failing to provide a centralized, un-overrideable preventative control. Option D is incorrect because Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior. It is a detective control and does not prevent misconfigurations from occurring.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_73',
  'A company is developing a new application that processes sensitive customer data and stores it in an Amazon S3 bucket. The security team requires that access to this S3 bucket from specific Amazon EC2 instances be restricted to only allow read operations, and all other access from these instances must be denied. Additionally, the bucket policy must explicitly deny access if the request does not originate from a specific VPC endpoint. This ensures that data access is tightly controlled and confined to the private network. Which combination of IAM policies and S3 bucket policies should a Solutions Architect implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["An IAM role attached to the EC2 instances with `s3:GetObject` permissions for the bucket, and an S3 bucket policy that denies access if `aws:SourceVpc` is not the specific VPC endpoint.","An IAM role attached to the EC2 instances with `s3:*` permissions, and an S3 bucket policy that allows `s3:GetObject` and denies all other actions from the EC2 instance''s IP address.","An S3 bucket policy that allows `s3:GetObject` from the EC2 instance''s IAM role and denies all other actions, without using a VPC endpoint condition.","A Security Group on the EC2 instances allowing outbound traffic to S3, and a Network ACL on the S3 bucket to restrict inbound access."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) provides the most secure and granular control. An IAM role attached to the EC2 instances grants specific `s3:GetObject` (read-only) permissions, adhering to the principle of least privilege. The S3 bucket policy then adds an additional layer of security by explicitly denying access if the request does not originate from the specified VPC endpoint (using the `aws:SourceVpc` condition key). This ensures that even if the IAM role is compromised, access is still restricted to the private network via the VPC endpoint, fulfilling both requirements. Option B is incorrect because granting `s3:*` permissions to the IAM role violates the principle of least privilege. While the bucket policy attempts to restrict, the initial broad permission is a security risk. Also, restricting by EC2 instance IP address is less dynamic and robust than using a VPC endpoint condition, especially in auto-scaling environments. Option C is incorrect because while an S3 bucket policy can allow `s3:GetObject` from an IAM role, it does not include the critical condition to deny access if the request does not originate from a specific VPC endpoint, failing to meet the requirement for private network confinement. Option D is incorrect because Security Groups control network traffic to/from EC2 instances, not directly to S3 buckets. S3 buckets do not have Network ACLs; NACLs operate at the subnet level within a VPC. This option demonstrates a misunderstanding of how S3 access and network security work together.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_74',
  'A media company uses Amazon S3 to store large video files that are accessed by a web application running on Amazon EC2 instances. The security team has identified that the EC2 instances currently have an IAM role with `s3:*` permissions, which violates the principle of least privilege. The team wants to restrict access to only the specific S3 bucket and only allow `s3:GetObject` and `s3:PutObject` actions. Additionally, they want to ensure that any future attempts to grant broader S3 permissions to these instances are prevented. Which combination of IAM policy and S3 bucket policy should a Solutions Architect implement to achieve this with the highest level of control?',
  'mcq',
  '["Update the IAM role attached to the EC2 instances to allow `s3:GetObject` and `s3:PutObject` on the specific S3 bucket, and add an S3 bucket policy that explicitly denies all other S3 actions for that role.","Create a new IAM user with restricted S3 permissions and configure the EC2 instances to use its access keys, then remove the existing IAM role.","Modify the existing IAM role to explicitly deny all S3 actions, and then rely solely on an S3 bucket policy to grant `s3:GetObject` and `s3:PutObject` to the EC2 instances.","Attach an IAM policy to the EC2 instances that allows `s3:GetObject` and `s3:PutObject` to the specific S3 bucket, and configure AWS Config to flag any `s3:*` permissions."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) provides the strongest and most secure implementation of least privilege for this scenario. By updating the IAM role attached to the EC2 instances to only allow `s3:GetObject` and `s3:PutObject` on the specific S3 bucket, you directly enforce least privilege at the identity level. Adding an S3 bucket policy that explicitly denies all other S3 actions for that role provides a defense-in-depth mechanism. This explicit deny in the bucket policy ensures that even if the IAM role''s permissions were accidentally broadened later, the bucket policy would still prevent unauthorized actions, providing the highest level of control. Option B is incorrect because using IAM user access keys on EC2 instances is an anti-pattern ([ap-1.1-002]) as it involves long-term credentials that are difficult to rotate and manage securely, increasing the risk of exposure. IAM roles are the recommended approach for EC2 instances. Option C is incorrect because relying solely on an S3 bucket policy to grant permissions when the IAM role explicitly denies all S3 actions would result in a complete denial of access. An explicit deny in an IAM policy takes precedence over an allow in a resource policy, making this configuration ineffective. Option D is incorrect because while attaching an IAM policy with restricted permissions is a good start, relying on AWS Config to flag `s3:*` permissions is a detective control, not a preventative one. It doesn''t prevent broader permissions from being granted in the first place, which contradicts the requirement to prevent future attempts to grant broader permissions with the highest level of control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_75',
  'A company is building a new application that requires users to sign up and sign in using their email addresses and passwords. Once authenticated, these users need to access specific AWS resources, such as uploading files to an Amazon S3 bucket or interacting with an Amazon DynamoDB table. The solution must provide a managed authentication service and a mechanism to grant temporary, limited-privilege access to AWS services for authenticated users, without exposing long-term AWS credentials. Which combination of AWS services should a Solutions Architect recommend?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Cognito User Pools for user authentication and Amazon Cognito Identity Pools for federating access to AWS resources.","AWS IAM Identity Center (successor to AWS SSO) for user authentication and IAM roles for granting access to AWS resources.","AWS IAM for creating individual user accounts and assigning specific permissions to access Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB.","A custom identity provider running on Amazon EC2, integrating with AWS Security Token Service (STS) for temporary credentials."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the ideal solution for customer-facing applications. Amazon Cognito User Pools provide a managed service for user sign-up, sign-in, and directory management. Amazon Cognito Identity Pools (federated identities) then allow authenticated users from a User Pool (or other identity providers) to obtain temporary, limited-privilege AWS credentials, enabling them to access specified AWS resources like Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB. This meets all requirements for managed authentication and secure, temporary access to AWS services. Option B is incorrect because AWS IAM Identity Center (successor to AWS SSO) is primarily designed for workforce identity management, providing SSO to AWS accounts and business applications for employees, not typically for customer-facing applications. Option C is incorrect because creating individual IAM users for application customers is not scalable or secure. IAM users are meant for AWS administrators and developers, not for application end-users. It also doesn''t provide a managed authentication service for sign-up/sign-in. Option D is incorrect because building a custom identity provider on Amazon EC2 introduces significant operational overhead for development, maintenance, scaling, and security, which contradicts the benefit of using managed services. While it can integrate with STS, it''s a much more complex approach than using Amazon Cognito.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_76',
  'A company is using AWS Organizations to manage multiple AWS accounts. The security team wants to ensure that all member accounts have AWS CloudTrail enabled and configured to log to a central S3 bucket in a dedicated logging account. This configuration must be enforced across all accounts, and no member account administrator should be able to disable or alter their CloudTrail configuration. Which AWS Organizations feature should a Solutions Architect use to enforce this preventative control?',
  'mcq',
  '["Service Control Policies (SCPs) applied to the root of the organization, explicitly denying actions to disable or modify CloudTrail trails.","AWS Config rules deployed in each member account to detect and remediate non-compliant CloudTrail configurations.","IAM policies attached to the IAM roles within each member account, denying CloudTrail modification actions.","An AWS Lambda function triggered by CloudTrail events to re-enable any disabled trails in member accounts."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective solution for enforcing preventative controls across an organization. Service Control Policies (SCPs) in AWS Organizations allow you to centrally manage permissions for all accounts. An SCP can explicitly deny actions such as `cloudtrail:StopLogging`, `cloudtrail:DeleteTrail`, or `cloudtrail:UpdateTrail` for all member accounts, ensuring that administrators cannot disable or alter their CloudTrail configurations. SCPs are preventative and cannot be overridden by IAM policies within member accounts. Option B is incorrect because AWS Config rules are detective controls; they detect non-compliance *after* it has occurred and can initiate remediation, but they do not prevent the action from happening in the first place. The requirement is for a preventative control. Option C is incorrect because IAM policies are managed within each account and can be modified or circumvented by administrators in those accounts with sufficient permissions, failing to provide the centralized, un-overrideable control required by the security team. Option D is incorrect because an AWS Lambda function triggered by CloudTrail events would be a reactive, detective, and auto-remediation approach. It would not prevent the disabling or alteration of trails, only attempt to fix it after the fact, which does not meet the preventative control requirement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_77',
  'Which AWS IAM best practice should a Solutions Architect recommend to prevent accidental broad access and enhance security for daily administrative tasks?',
  'mcq',
  '["Create individual AWS IAM users with specific permissions and use AWS IAM roles for temporary access.","Use the AWS account root user credentials for all daily administrative operations to ensure full access.","Share a single AWS IAM user account with administrative privileges among all team members.","Embed AWS account root user access keys directly into application code for programmatic access."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) aligns with the principle of least privilege and AWS best practices. Creating individual IAM users with specific, limited permissions and using IAM roles for temporary, elevated access ensures that users only have the necessary access to perform their tasks, reducing the risk of accidental or malicious over-privilege. Option B (incorrect) is an anti-pattern; the AWS account root user has unrestricted access and should only be used for a few account and service management tasks, not for daily operations. Option C (incorrect) violates the principle of individual accountability and makes auditing difficult, as it''s impossible to trace actions back to a specific individual. Option D (incorrect) is a severe security anti-pattern; embedding any long-term access keys, especially root user keys, in application code risks exposure and compromise, and is difficult to rotate.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_78',
  'A company wants to centralize access management to multiple AWS accounts and business applications, allowing employees to sign in once using their existing corporate directory credentials. Which AWS service is designed for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS IAM","AWS IAM Identity Center (formerly AWS SSO)","Amazon Cognito","AWS Organizations"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS IAM Identity Center, which provides a single sign-on (SSO) experience for users to access multiple AWS accounts and cloud applications. It integrates with existing corporate directories like Active Directory or Okta, enabling centralized identity management. Option A (incorrect) is AWS IAM, which manages users, groups, roles, and permissions within a single AWS account, but does not inherently provide a centralized SSO experience across multiple accounts or external applications. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon Cognito, which is primarily used for customer-facing applications to manage user sign-up, sign-in, and access control, not for enterprise workforce identity federation across AWS accounts. Option D (incorrect) is AWS Organizations, which helps you centrally manage and govern your environment as you grow and scale your AWS resources, but it doesn''t provide the identity federation and single sign-on capabilities for users to access these accounts.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_18_2
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_56',
  'Which AWS service helps protect your web applications from common web exploits and bots?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Shield","AWS WAF","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Config"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS WAF) is a web application firewall that helps protect web applications or APIs from common web exploits that may affect availability, compromise security, or consume excessive resources. Option A (AWS Shield) provides DDoS protection. Option C (Amazon GuardDuty) is a threat detection service. Option D (AWS Config) assesses, audits, and evaluates the configurations of your AWS resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_57',
  'What is the primary function of an Amazon VPC security group?',
  'mcq',
  '["To filter traffic at the subnet level (stateless)","To control inbound and outbound traffic for Amazon EC2 instances (stateful)","To provide a private connection to AWS services","To encrypt data at rest within a VPC"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (To control inbound and outbound traffic for Amazon EC2 instances (stateful)) accurately describes the primary function of a security group. Security groups act as a virtual firewall for your EC2 instances. Option A (To filter traffic at the subnet level (stateless)) describes a Network ACL. Option C describes AWS PrivateLink or VPC Endpoints. Option D is related to encryption services like AWS KMS, not security groups.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_58',
  'Which service should a Solutions Architect recommend for securely storing and automatically rotating database credentials?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store","AWS Secrets Manager","Amazon S3","Environment variables"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Secrets Manager) is the ideal service for securely storing and automatically rotating database credentials, API keys, and other secrets. Option A (AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store) can store secrets as SecureString, but it does not offer automatic rotation. Option C (Amazon S3) is object storage and not designed for credential management. Option D (Environment variables) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.2-002) as it exposes credentials and lacks rotation capabilities.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_59',
  'A company needs to restrict SSH access to their Amazon EC2 instances to only specific IP addresses. Which AWS networking component should be configured?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Route 53","Network ACLs","Security Groups","VPC Endpoints"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Security Groups) are stateful virtual firewalls that control inbound and outbound traffic for Amazon EC2 instances. They are the appropriate tool to restrict SSH access to specific IP addresses. Option A (Amazon Route 53) is a DNS service. Option B (Network ACLs) are stateless firewalls at the subnet level, which could also be used but security groups are more granular for instance-level control. Option D (VPC Endpoints) provide private connectivity to AWS services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_60',
  'What is the purpose of Amazon Cognito User Pools?',
  'mcq',
  '["To provide temporary AWS credentials for authenticated users","To manage user directories and enable sign-up/sign-in functionality for web and mobile apps","To store application configuration data","To encrypt data at rest for mobile applications"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (To manage user directories and enable sign-up/sign-in functionality for web and mobile apps) is the primary purpose of Amazon Cognito User Pools. They handle user registration, authentication, and account management. Option A (To provide temporary AWS credentials for authenticated users) describes Amazon Cognito Identity Pools (anti-pattern ap-1.1-004). Option C describes AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store. Option D is not a direct function of Cognito User Pools.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_61',
  'Which AWS service is used to establish a private connection between your on-premises data center and your Amazon VPC?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration","AWS Direct Connect","Amazon CloudFront","AWS VPN Client"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Direct Connect) is a cloud service solution that makes it easy to establish a dedicated network connection from your premises to AWS, providing a private, consistent, and lower-latency connection. Option A (Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration) speeds up data transfers to S3. Option C (Amazon CloudFront) is a content delivery network. Option D (AWS VPN Client) is for client-based VPN access, not site-to-site dedicated connections.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_62',
  'Which AWS service provides free SSL/TLS certificates for securing public-facing AWS resources like Elastic Load Balancers and Amazon CloudFront distributions?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS KMS","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS CloudHSM","AWS Secrets Manager"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)) is the correct service. ACM provides free SSL/TLS certificates that can be easily provisioned, managed, and deployed on AWS services like Elastic Load Balancers, Amazon CloudFront, and API Gateway. Option A (AWS KMS) is for managing encryption keys. Option C (AWS CloudHSM) provides dedicated hardware security modules. Option D (AWS Secrets Manager) stores and rotates secrets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_63',
  'What is the primary benefit of using AWS PrivateLink?',
  'mcq',
  '["To accelerate data transfer to Amazon S3","To provide private connectivity between VPCs and AWS services without using the public internet","To distribute incoming application traffic across multiple targets","To monitor network traffic for security threats"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (To provide private connectivity between VPCs and AWS services without using the public internet) is the primary benefit of AWS PrivateLink. It allows you to access AWS services and VPC-hosted services securely and privately from your VPC. Option A (To accelerate data transfer to Amazon S3) is a function of S3 Transfer Acceleration. Option C (To distribute incoming application traffic across multiple targets) describes an Elastic Load Balancer. Option D (To monitor network traffic for security threats) is a function of services like Amazon GuardDuty or VPC Flow Logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_64',
  'Which AWS service helps protect against common distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attacks?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Config","AWS Shield","Amazon Inspector","AWS KMS"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Shield) is a managed DDoS protection service that safeguards applications running on AWS. It provides always-on detection and automatic inline mitigations. Option A (AWS Config) assesses resource configurations. Option C (Amazon Inspector) is for vulnerability assessment. Option D (AWS KMS) is for key management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_65',
  'A company needs to protect its public-facing web application running on Amazon EC2 instances from common web exploits like SQL injection and cross-site scripting. Which AWS service should be used?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS WAF","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Shield Advanced","Amazon Inspector"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS WAF (Web Application Firewall). AWS WAF helps protect web applications from common web exploits that could affect application availability, compromise security, or consume excessive resources. It allows you to create custom rules to filter web traffic based on conditions like SQL injection or cross-site scripting. Option B (incorrect) is Amazon GuardDuty, which is a threat detection service that continuously monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior to protect AWS accounts and workloads. It does not actively block web exploits. Option C (incorrect) is AWS Shield Advanced, which provides enhanced DDoS protection for applications. While important for security, it focuses on volumetric and state-exhaustion DDoS attacks, not application-layer exploits like SQL injection. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon Inspector, which is an automated security assessment service that helps improve the security and compliance of applications deployed on AWS by identifying vulnerabilities.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_66',
  'An application running on Amazon EC2 instances in a private subnet needs to access the internet to download software updates, but direct inbound internet access to the instances must be prevented. Which AWS networking component should be used?',
  'mcq',
  '["NAT Gateway","Internet Gateway","VPC Endpoint","AWS PrivateLink"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is a NAT Gateway. A NAT Gateway allows instances in a private subnet to connect to the internet or other AWS services, but it prevents the internet from initiating a connection with those instances. This meets the requirement for outbound internet access without inbound access. Option B (incorrect) is an Internet Gateway, which allows direct two-way communication between instances in a public subnet and the internet. This would allow inbound internet access, violating the requirement. Option C (incorrect) is a VPC Endpoint, which enables private connectivity to AWS services without requiring an Internet Gateway, NAT device, VPN connection, or AWS Direct Connect. It''s for accessing AWS services privately, not for general internet access. Option D (incorrect) is AWS PrivateLink, which provides private connectivity between VPCs, AWS services, and on-premises applications, similar to VPC Endpoints but specifically for services you host or consume from other VPCs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_67',
  'A Solutions Architect needs to implement a stateless firewall at the subnet level to filter traffic entering and leaving the subnet. Which AWS networking component should be configured?',
  'mcq',
  '["Network Access Control List (NACL)","Security Group","AWS WAF","Amazon GuardDuty"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is a Network Access Control List (NACL). NACLs operate at the subnet level, providing a stateless firewall that filters traffic based on rules for both inbound and outbound connections. They are stateless, meaning that if you allow an inbound rule, you must also explicitly allow the outbound response. Option B (incorrect) is a Security Group, which acts as a stateful firewall at the instance level. Security groups automatically allow return traffic for any allowed inbound or outbound rule. Option C (incorrect) is AWS WAF, which is a web application firewall that protects against common web exploits at the application layer, not at the network subnet level. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon GuardDuty, which is a threat detection service, not a firewall.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_68',
  'A company needs to securely store database credentials for multiple applications, ensuring automatic rotation and integration with various AWS services. Which AWS service is best suited for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store","Amazon S3","AWS Key Management Service (KMS)"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS Secrets Manager. It helps you protect access to your applications, services, and IT resources by enabling you to easily rotate, manage, and retrieve database credentials, API keys, and other secrets throughout their lifecycle. It offers automatic rotation for many database types. Option B (incorrect) is AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store, which provides secure, hierarchical storage for configuration data and secrets (using SecureString). While it can store secrets, it does not offer automatic rotation capabilities like Secrets Manager. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon S3, which is object storage and not designed for secure, rotatable secret management. Storing credentials directly in S3 is not a best practice. Option D (incorrect) is AWS Key Management Service (KMS), which is used to create and manage cryptographic keys and control their use across a wide range of AWS services. While Secrets Manager uses KMS for encryption, KMS itself does not store or rotate the secrets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_69',
  'A developer needs to configure a security group for an Amazon EC2 instance that hosts a web server. The web server should only be accessible via HTTP (port 80) and HTTPS (port 443) from the internet. Which inbound rules should be configured?',
  'mcq',
  '["Allow inbound TCP port 80 from 0.0.0.0/0 and inbound TCP port 443 from 0.0.0.0/0.","Allow inbound TCP port 80 from 10.0.0.0/16 and inbound TCP port 443 from 10.0.0.0/16.","Allow inbound TCP port 22 from 0.0.0.0/0 and inbound TCP port 80 from 0.0.0.0/0.","Deny all inbound traffic except for TCP port 80 and TCP port 443."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the appropriate configuration. To make a web server accessible from the entire internet via HTTP and HTTPS, you must allow inbound traffic on TCP port 80 and TCP port 443 from the source IP address range 0.0.0.0/0 (which represents all IP addresses). Option B (incorrect) would only allow access from a private IP range (10.0.0.0/16), which would prevent internet access. Option C (incorrect) would open SSH (port 22) to the entire internet, which is an anti-pattern ([ap-1.2-001]) and a significant security risk, and it doesn''t include HTTPS (port 443). Option D (incorrect) is not how security groups work. Security groups are allow-only; you cannot explicitly deny traffic. Instead, you define what is allowed, and everything else is implicitly denied.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_70',
  'Which AWS service provides free, auto-renewing SSL/TLS certificates for securing public-facing AWS services like Elastic Load Balancing and Amazon CloudFront?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS Key Management Service (KMS)","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS CloudHSM"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS Certificate Manager (ACM). ACM handles the complexity of creating, storing, and renewing SSL/TLS certificates for your AWS-provisioned websites and applications. It provides free, auto-renewing certificates for use with integrated AWS services. Option B (incorrect) is AWS Key Management Service (KMS), which is used for creating and managing cryptographic keys, not for issuing SSL/TLS certificates. Option C (incorrect) is AWS Secrets Manager, which is used for managing and rotating secrets like database credentials, not for SSL/TLS certificates. Option D (incorrect) is AWS CloudHSM, which provides dedicated hardware security modules (HSMs) in the AWS Cloud for cryptographic operations and key storage, but it does not issue public SSL/TLS certificates.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_71',
  'A company requires a private, secure connection from their on-premises data center to their Amazon VPC, bypassing the public internet. Which AWS service can achieve this with dedicated network connectivity?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Direct Connect","AWS Site-to-Site VPN","VPC Peering","AWS PrivateLink"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS Direct Connect. AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated network connection from your premises to AWS, bypassing the public internet. This provides a more consistent network experience than internet-based connections. Option B (incorrect) is AWS Site-to-Site VPN, which creates an encrypted connection over the public internet between your data center and your VPC. While secure, it still uses the public internet. Option C (incorrect) is VPC Peering, which connects two VPCs together so that they can communicate as if they are within the same network. It does not connect an on-premises data center to a VPC. Option D (incorrect) is AWS PrivateLink, which provides private connectivity between VPCs and AWS services or services hosted by other AWS accounts, not for connecting on-premises data centers to a VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_72',
  'Which AWS service helps protect your web applications or APIs against common web exploits and bots that may affect availability, compromise security, or consume excessive resources?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS WAF","AWS Shield","Amazon GuardDuty","Amazon Inspector"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS WAF (Web Application Firewall). AWS WAF allows you to monitor the HTTP(S) requests that are forwarded to an Amazon CloudFront distribution, an Amazon API Gateway REST API, or an Application Load Balancer. It lets you control access to your content based on conditions that you specify, such as IP addresses, HTTP headers, HTTP body, or URI strings, and protects against common web exploits. Option B (incorrect) is AWS Shield, which is a managed Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) protection service. While important for security, it focuses on DDoS attacks, not application-layer exploits. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon GuardDuty, which is a threat detection service that continuously monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior. It detects threats but does not actively block them like a firewall. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon Inspector, which is an automated security assessment service that helps improve the security and compliance of applications deployed on AWS by identifying vulnerabilities.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_73',
  'A company wants to identify potential security vulnerabilities in their Amazon EC2 instances and installed applications. Which AWS service can automate this security assessment?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Inspector","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Security Hub","Amazon Macie"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon Inspector. Amazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that helps improve the security and compliance of applications deployed on AWS. It automatically discovers and scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure. Option B (incorrect) is Amazon GuardDuty, which is a threat detection service that continuously monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior, rather than performing vulnerability assessments on instances. Option C (incorrect) is AWS Security Hub, which provides a comprehensive view of your security alerts and security posture across your AWS accounts, but it aggregates findings from other services, rather than performing the direct assessment itself. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon Macie, which is a data security and data privacy service that uses machine learning and pattern matching to discover and protect sensitive data in AWS, primarily in Amazon S3.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_74',
  'What is the primary function of a Security Group in an Amazon VPC?',
  'mcq',
  '["To act as a stateful firewall for EC2 instances","To act as a stateless firewall for subnets","To route traffic between subnets","To provide private connectivity to AWS services"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the primary function of a Security Group. A Security Group acts as a virtual stateful firewall for your EC2 instances to control inbound and outbound traffic. It evaluates all rules before allowing or denying traffic, and automatically allows return traffic for any allowed connection. Option B (incorrect) describes a Network Access Control List (NACL), which is a stateless firewall at the subnet level. Option C (incorrect) describes a Route Table, which is used to define rules for routing network traffic from subnets. Option D (incorrect) describes a VPC Endpoint or AWS PrivateLink, which provide private connectivity to AWS services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_75',
  'A development team is building a new microservices application that will run on Amazon EC2 instances within a private subnet. The application needs to securely access an Amazon S3 bucket to store and retrieve configuration files and an Amazon DynamoDB table for session management. The security team mandates that all traffic to AWS services must remain within the AWS network and not traverse the public internet, even if the services are public endpoints. Additionally, the solution must be highly available and scalable. Which networking configuration should a Solutions Architect implement to meet these requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure VPC Endpoints (Gateway Endpoint for Amazon S3 and Interface Endpoint for Amazon DynamoDB) within the VPC.","Deploy a NAT Gateway in a public subnet to allow instances in the private subnet to access Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB.","Use an Internet Gateway attached to the VPC and configure route tables to direct traffic to Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB.","Create a VPN connection from the VPC to an on-premises data center, then route all AWS service traffic through the VPN."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the appropriate solution. VPC Endpoints allow private connectivity to AWS services from within a VPC without traversing the public internet. For Amazon S3, a Gateway Endpoint is used, and for Amazon DynamoDB, an Interface Endpoint (powered by AWS PrivateLink) is used. This ensures all traffic remains within the AWS network, meeting the security mandate, and provides a highly available and scalable solution. Option B is incorrect because a NAT Gateway allows instances in a private subnet to initiate outbound connections to the internet, including public AWS service endpoints, but the traffic would still traverse the public internet, violating the security mandate. Option C is incorrect because an Internet Gateway provides internet access to the VPC, meaning traffic to public AWS service endpoints would traverse the public internet, directly violating the security requirement. Option D is incorrect because routing AWS service traffic through an on-premises VPN connection introduces unnecessary latency and complexity, and it doesn''t keep the traffic entirely within the AWS network for direct AWS service access, making it inefficient and not the intended use case for securing direct AWS service access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_76',
  'A company is deploying a new web application on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB). The application must accept only HTTPS traffic on port 443 and requires a valid TLS certificate issued by a trusted Certificate Authority. The security team also requires that the TLS certificates be automatically renewed and managed with minimal operational effort. Which AWS service should a Solutions Architect use to provision and manage the TLS certificates for the ALB?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) to provision and manage public TLS certificates, which can then be associated with the Application Load Balancer.","Manually generate a self-signed certificate using OpenSSL on an Amazon EC2 instance and upload it to the Application Load Balancer.","Purchase a TLS certificate from a third-party Certificate Authority, then manually import it into AWS IAM and associate it with the Application Load Balancer.","Use AWS Secrets Manager to store the TLS certificate private key and then configure the Application Load Balancer to retrieve it dynamically."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most suitable and recommended solution. AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) allows you to easily provision, manage, and deploy public and private SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services like Application Load Balancers. ACM certificates are free for use with integrated services and automatically renew, significantly reducing operational overhead and ensuring valid, trusted certificates. Option B is incorrect because self-signed certificates are not trusted by browsers and will cause security warnings for users, eroding trust and violating the requirement for a valid TLS certificate issued by a trusted CA. This represents anti-pattern [ap-1.2-004]. Option C is incorrect because while importing a third-party certificate into IAM is possible, it requires manual renewal and management, increasing operational overhead compared to ACM''s automatic renewal, and doesn''t fully leverage AWS''s integrated certificate management capabilities. Option D is incorrect because AWS Secrets Manager is designed for storing and managing secrets like database credentials, API keys, or other sensitive information, not for directly provisioning and managing TLS certificates for an Application Load Balancer. While it could store parts of a certificate, it does not provide the automatic renewal and integration benefits of ACM.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_77',
  'A company is developing a new serverless application using AWS Lambda and Amazon API Gateway. The application needs to store sensitive user configuration data, such as API keys for third-party services, which must be encrypted at rest and in transit. The security team requires that these secrets be automatically rotated and securely retrieved by the Lambda functions at runtime, without hardcoding them into the Lambda code or environment variables. Which AWS service should a Solutions Architect recommend for managing these application secrets?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Secrets Manager, configured to store and automatically rotate the sensitive API keys, with Lambda functions retrieving them at runtime.","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store, using SecureString parameters to store the API keys, and Lambda functions retrieving them.","Environment variables within the AWS Lambda function configuration, storing the encrypted API keys directly.","An Amazon S3 bucket with server-side encryption enabled, storing the API keys in encrypted text files."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the ideal solution for this scenario. AWS Secrets Manager is specifically designed for managing, retrieving, and rotating database credentials, API keys, and other secrets throughout their lifecycle. It supports automatic rotation, which is a key requirement, and allows Lambda functions to securely retrieve secrets at runtime, preventing hardcoding and adhering to best practices. Option B is incorrect because while AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store SecureString can store encrypted parameters, it does not offer built-in automatic rotation for arbitrary secrets like API keys, which is a critical requirement from the security team. Option C is incorrect because storing sensitive API keys directly in Lambda environment variables, even if encrypted, is generally discouraged as they can be visible in the console and logs, and it does not provide automatic rotation. This represents anti-pattern [ap-1.2-002]. Option D is incorrect because storing API keys in encrypted text files in Amazon S3, while providing encryption at rest, lacks the automatic rotation capabilities and secure retrieval mechanisms specifically designed for application secrets that AWS Secrets Manager offers. It would also require custom logic for retrieval and rotation, increasing operational overhead.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_78',
  'A company is hosting a public-facing web application on Amazon EC2 instances within a VPC. The application experiences frequent web-based attacks, including SQL injection attempts and cross-site scripting (XSS). The security team needs a solution to protect the application from these common web exploits and also mitigate large-scale DDoS attacks at the network and application layers. The solution must be managed and integrated seamlessly with the existing Application Load Balancer (ALB). Which combination of AWS services should a Solutions Architect recommend?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS WAF for protection against web exploits and AWS Shield Advanced for DDoS protection, both integrated with the Application Load Balancer.","Amazon GuardDuty to detect web exploits and AWS Shield Standard for DDoS protection, with custom rules on Security Groups.","Network ACLs (NACLs) to block malicious IP addresses and Amazon Inspector to identify web vulnerabilities on EC2 instances.","Implement a custom intrusion detection system (IDS) on an Amazon EC2 instance and use AWS Systems Manager to manage it."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most comprehensive and integrated solution. AWS WAF (Web Application Firewall) is designed to protect web applications from common web exploits like SQL injection and XSS by allowing you to create custom rules or use managed rule sets. AWS Shield Advanced provides enhanced DDoS protection for applications, including layer 3/4 (network) and layer 7 (application) attacks, with 24/7 DDoS response team access. Both services integrate seamlessly with Application Load Balancers, providing a robust, managed security layer. Option B is incorrect because Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior, but it does not actively prevent web exploits or DDoS attacks. AWS Shield Standard provides basic DDoS protection but lacks the advanced features and response team of Shield Advanced, and relying solely on Security Groups for complex web exploit mitigation is insufficient. Option C is incorrect because Network ACLs are stateless and operate at the subnet level, making them inefficient for blocking specific web exploits or managing dynamic malicious IP lists. Amazon Inspector is a vulnerability management service that identifies security vulnerabilities but does not provide real-time protection against active attacks. Option D is incorrect because implementing and managing a custom IDS on an Amazon EC2 instance introduces significant operational overhead, including maintenance, scaling, and rule updates, which contradicts the goal of a managed and seamlessly integrated solution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_79',
  'A large enterprise has a critical application running on Amazon EC2 instances in a private subnet. The application needs to connect to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database instance, also in a private subnet. The security team mandates that all network traffic between the application and the database must be encrypted in transit and isolated from the public internet. Furthermore, the database must only be accessible from the specific EC2 instances running the application, adhering strictly to the principle of least privilege for network access. Which network security configurations should a Solutions Architect implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Security Groups for the EC2 instances to allow outbound traffic to the RDS Security Group, and configure the RDS Security Group to allow inbound traffic from the EC2 instance Security Group, ensuring both are in private subnets.","Use Network ACLs (NACLs) on the subnets containing the EC2 instances and RDS database to explicitly deny all traffic except on the PostgreSQL port (5432) between them.","Deploy an AWS PrivateLink endpoint for the Amazon RDS database, allowing the EC2 instances to connect privately without public internet exposure.","Set up a VPN connection between the EC2 instances and the Amazon RDS database, ensuring encrypted communication over the private network."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective and standard approach for securing communication between EC2 instances and an RDS database within a VPC. By configuring Security Groups, you can precisely control inbound and outbound traffic at the instance level. The EC2 instance Security Group allows outbound traffic to the RDS Security Group, and the RDS Security Group allows inbound traffic from the EC2 instance Security Group. Placing both in private subnets ensures isolation from the public internet. RDS automatically supports SSL/TLS for encryption in transit. Option B is incorrect because Network ACLs (NACLs) are stateless and operate at the subnet level. While they can filter traffic, using them for granular instance-level access control between application and database is less flexible and more complex to manage than Security Groups, especially for least privilege. NACLs are typically used for broader subnet-level filtering. Option C is incorrect because AWS PrivateLink is used to provide private connectivity to services hosted by AWS or other AWS customers, often for services that don''t inherently reside within your VPC (like SaaS offerings). Amazon RDS instances are already deployed within your VPC, making PrivateLink unnecessary and not the primary mechanism for securing internal VPC traffic to RDS. Option D is incorrect because setting up a VPN connection *between* EC2 instances and an RDS database within the same VPC is entirely unnecessary and overly complex. Both services are already within the same private network, and their communication is secured via Security Groups and SSL/TLS for encryption in transit.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_80',
  'A company is hosting a multi-tier web application in a VPC. The web tier (public subnet) communicates with the application tier (private subnet), which in turn communicates with a database tier (private subnet). The security team requires strict inbound and outbound traffic filtering at both the instance level and the subnet level to achieve defense-in-depth. Specifically, they want to block specific malicious IP addresses at the subnet boundary and ensure that only necessary ports are open between tiers. Which combination of AWS networking features should a Solutions Architect implement to meet these requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Security Groups for each EC2 instance to control traffic between tiers, and Network ACLs (NACLs) for each subnet to block specific malicious IP addresses.","Use only Security Groups for all traffic filtering, as they are stateful and provide granular control at the instance level.","Deploy an AWS WAF on an Application Load Balancer in the public subnet to block malicious IPs, and use Security Groups for internal communication.","Implement a custom firewall on an Amazon EC2 instance in the public subnet to filter all inbound and outbound traffic."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) provides the required defense-in-depth. Security Groups operate at the instance level, are stateful, and are ideal for controlling traffic between different application tiers (e.g., web to app, app to DB) by allowing only necessary ports. Network ACLs (NACLs) operate at the subnet level, are stateless, and can be used to explicitly allow or deny specific IP addresses or ranges at the subnet boundary, which is perfect for blocking known malicious IPs. This combination provides both instance-level and subnet-level filtering, achieving defense-in-depth. Option B is incorrect because relying solely on Security Groups, while providing instance-level control, does not offer the additional stateless filtering layer at the subnet level that NACLs provide, which is crucial for defense-in-depth and explicitly blocking IPs at the subnet boundary. This represents anti-pattern [ap-1.2-003]. Option C is incorrect because AWS WAF operates at the application layer (Layer 7) and is typically used with ALBs to protect against web exploits, not for general network traffic filtering between VPC subnets or blocking IPs at the network layer (Layer 3/4). While it can block IPs, it''s not the primary tool for subnet-level network access control. Option D is incorrect because implementing a custom firewall on an Amazon EC2 instance introduces significant operational overhead for deployment, configuration, scaling, and maintenance, which is generally not recommended when AWS provides managed network security features like Security Groups and NACLs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_81',
  'A company needs to implement a secure and scalable solution for storing application configuration data and secrets, such as database connection strings and API keys. These secrets must be encrypted at rest, versioned, and accessible by multiple AWS Lambda functions and Amazon EC2 instances. The security team mandates that the solution must support automatic rotation for database credentials and provide a clear audit trail of secret access. Which combination of AWS services should a Solutions Architect recommend to meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Secrets Manager for database credentials and API keys with automatic rotation, and AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store SecureString for other configuration data.","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store SecureString for all secrets and configuration data, and implement custom Lambda functions for rotation.","Store all secrets and configuration data in encrypted Amazon S3 buckets, with S3 Versioning enabled for history.","Hardcode secrets and configuration data as environment variables in AWS Lambda and EC2 user data, encrypting them with AWS KMS."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) provides the optimal solution with the least operational overhead. AWS Secrets Manager is specifically designed for managing, retrieving, and automatically rotating database credentials and API keys, meeting the rotation requirement and providing an audit trail. AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store SecureString is excellent for storing other sensitive configuration data that doesn''t require automatic rotation, offering encryption and versioning. This combination leverages the strengths of both services for different types of secrets/configuration. Option B is incorrect because while AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store SecureString provides encryption and versioning, it does not offer built-in automatic rotation for database credentials or API keys. Implementing custom Lambda functions for rotation would increase operational overhead, which contradicts the requirement. Option C is incorrect because while encrypted Amazon S3 buckets with versioning can store data, S3 is not designed as a secrets management service with features like automatic rotation, secure retrieval mechanisms for applications, or direct integration for database credential rotation. It would require significant custom development. Option D is incorrect because hardcoding secrets as environment variables or in user data is a severe security anti-pattern ([ap-1.2-002]), even if encrypted, as they are easily exposed and do not support automatic rotation, violating multiple security requirements.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_82',
  'A company is deploying a new application that needs to securely store and retrieve sensitive configuration parameters, such as API endpoints and feature flags, which are not considered high-value secrets like database credentials. These parameters must be encrypted at rest, versioned, and easily accessible by various AWS services like AWS Lambda and Amazon EC2 instances. The solution should be cost-effective and require minimal management overhead. Which AWS service should a Solutions Architect recommend for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store, using SecureString for sensitive parameters and Standard parameters for non-sensitive ones.","AWS Secrets Manager, storing all configuration parameters as secrets with automatic rotation disabled.","Amazon S3, storing configuration parameters in encrypted text files with S3 Versioning enabled.","AWS CloudHSM, storing encryption keys for the configuration parameters in dedicated hardware security modules."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most suitable and cost-effective solution for storing application configuration parameters. AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store provides a hierarchical storage for configuration data management and secrets management. It supports SecureString for sensitive parameters (encrypted using AWS KMS) and Standard parameters for non-sensitive ones, offering versioning and easy access by other AWS services. It is highly available, scalable, and cost-effective with minimal management overhead. Option B is incorrect because AWS Secrets Manager is designed for high-value secrets like database credentials that often require automatic rotation. While it can store other parameters, it is generally more expensive than Parameter Store and overkill for general configuration data that doesn''t require rotation, thus not meeting the cost-effective requirement. Option C is incorrect because while Amazon S3 can store encrypted files with versioning, it is an object storage service and not optimized for direct, programmatic retrieval of individual configuration parameters by applications in the same way Parameter Store is. It would require custom parsing and management. Option D is incorrect because AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated hardware security modules for highly sensitive cryptographic key management (FIPS 140-2 Level 3). It is a high-cost, high-control service and completely disproportionate for storing application configuration parameters, which are not encryption keys themselves.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_83',
  'A company is developing a new application that will be deployed on Amazon EC2 instances. The application needs to securely connect to an external, on-premises database. The security team mandates that all traffic between the AWS VPC and the on-premises network must be encrypted, private, and highly available, without traversing the public internet. The company has a dedicated network connection to AWS. Which AWS service should a Solutions Architect recommend to establish this secure and private connectivity?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Direct Connect, combined with a VPN connection over the Direct Connect link for encryption.","AWS Site-to-Site VPN, establishing an encrypted tunnel over the public internet.","VPC Peering between the application VPC and a dedicated network VPC that has internet access.","An Internet Gateway attached to the VPC, with Security Groups configured to allow specific database ports."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most appropriate solution. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated, private network connection from an on-premises data center to AWS, bypassing the public internet. To ensure encryption in transit over this private connection, a VPN connection (either AWS Site-to-Site VPN or a customer-managed VPN) can be established over the Direct Connect link. This combination meets all requirements for encrypted, private, and highly available connectivity without traversing the public internet. Option B is incorrect because AWS Site-to-Site VPN establishes an encrypted tunnel over the *public internet*. While it provides encryption, it does not meet the requirement of *not* traversing the public internet for the underlying connection. Option C is incorrect because VPC Peering connects two VPCs within AWS, not an AWS VPC to an on-premises network. It is irrelevant for hybrid cloud connectivity. Option D is incorrect because an Internet Gateway provides connectivity to the public internet. While Security Groups can control traffic, using an Internet Gateway would mean the traffic traverses the public internet, violating a key security mandate.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_84',
  'A development team needs to securely store and automatically rotate database credentials for an application running on Amazon EC2 instances. Which AWS service should be used to meet this requirement with minimal operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Store credentials as environment variables on the Amazon EC2 instances.","Use AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store SecureString for encrypted storage.","Implement AWS Secrets Manager for credential storage and automatic rotation.","Hardcode credentials directly into the application''s source code."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS Secrets Manager, which is specifically designed for securely storing and automatically rotating database credentials, API keys, and other secrets. It integrates with various AWS services and databases to automate the rotation process, significantly reducing operational overhead. Option A (incorrect) is an anti-pattern; storing credentials in environment variables makes them visible in console outputs, logs, and potentially accessible to other processes on the instance, lacking robust security and rotation capabilities. Option B (incorrect) is AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store SecureString, which can securely store encrypted parameters, but it does not offer built-in automatic rotation capabilities for database credentials, requiring custom automation. Option D (incorrect) is a severe security anti-pattern; hardcoding credentials in source code is highly insecure, makes rotation difficult, and risks exposure through version control systems.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
