INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_64',
  'A company needs to ensure that all data uploaded to an Amazon S3 bucket is encrypted at rest by default. Which S3 feature should be enabled?',
  'mcq',
  '["S3 Versioning","S3 Transfer Acceleration","Default encryption for the S3 bucket","S3 Object Lock"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Default encryption for the S3 bucket) is the correct feature to ensure all new objects uploaded to an S3 bucket are encrypted at rest by default. You can choose between SSE-S3 or SSE-KMS for this. Option A (S3 Versioning) protects against accidental deletions. Option B (S3 Transfer Acceleration) speeds up data transfers. Option D (S3 Object Lock) enforces immutability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_65',
  'A company stores highly sensitive customer data in Amazon S3 and requires encryption at rest with full control over the encryption keys, including the ability to rotate them on demand and audit key usage. Which S3 encryption option should be chosen?',
  'mcq',
  '["Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS) using a customer-managed key (CMK)","Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3 managed keys (SSE-S3)","Server-Side Encryption with customer-provided keys (SSE-C)","Client-Side Encryption with a key managed outside of AWS"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS) using a customer-managed key (CMK). This option provides full control over the encryption keys, allowing customers to define key policies, rotate keys on demand, and audit key usage through AWS CloudTrail, which is crucial for compliance with strict regulatory requirements. Option B (incorrect) is Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3 managed keys (SSE-S3). While it encrypts data at rest, AWS manages the encryption keys, and customers have no control over key rotation or direct audit trails of key usage, which fails the requirement for full control. Option C (incorrect) is Server-Side Encryption with customer-provided keys (SSE-C). With SSE-C, the customer provides the encryption key, but AWS performs the encryption/decryption. The customer is responsible for managing the key, but KMS features like automatic rotation and detailed audit trails are not integrated as they are with SSE-KMS. Option D (incorrect) is Client-Side Encryption with a key managed outside of AWS. While this gives full control, it increases operational overhead significantly for key management and rotation, and doesn''t leverage the managed benefits of AWS KMS, making it less ideal for a ''most effective'' solution within AWS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_66',
  'A compliance requirement states that all data stored in Amazon S3 must be immutable for a specific period to prevent accidental deletion or modification. Which S3 feature should be enabled?',
  'mcq',
  '["S3 Object Lock","S3 Versioning","S3 Lifecycle Policies","S3 Cross-Region Replication"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is S3 Object Lock. S3 Object Lock prevents objects from being deleted or overwritten for a fixed amount of time or indefinitely, providing WORM (Write Once Read Many) capabilities. This directly addresses the requirement for immutability to meet compliance. Option B (incorrect) is S3 Versioning, which keeps multiple versions of an object in the same bucket. While it protects against accidental overwrites by keeping previous versions, it does not prevent the deletion of all versions or the bucket itself, and it doesn''t enforce immutability for compliance in the same way Object Lock does. S3 Object Lock requires versioning to be enabled, but versioning alone is not sufficient for immutability. Option C (incorrect) is S3 Lifecycle Policies, which automate the movement or deletion of objects based on age. It does not provide immutability. Option D (incorrect) is S3 Cross-Region Replication, which copies objects to a different AWS Region for disaster recovery. It does not provide immutability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_67',
  'Which AWS service is specifically designed to create and manage cryptographic keys and control their use across a wide range of AWS services and applications?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Key Management Service (KMS)","AWS CloudHSM","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS Key Management Service (KMS). KMS is a managed service that makes it easy for you to create and control the encryption keys used to encrypt your data. It integrates with many other AWS services to provide encryption at rest and in transit. Option B (incorrect) is AWS CloudHSM, which provides dedicated hardware security modules (HSMs) in the AWS Cloud. While it manages keys, it offers a higher level of FIPS compliance and direct control over the HSM, typically for very specific regulatory requirements, and involves more operational overhead than KMS. Option C (incorrect) is AWS Secrets Manager, which is used for managing and rotating secrets like database credentials, not primarily for cryptographic key management itself. Option D (incorrect) is AWS Certificate Manager (ACM), which manages SSL/TLS certificates for public-facing AWS services, not general cryptographic keys.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_68',
  'A company needs to encrypt data in transit for their web application. Which technology is commonly used to achieve this by establishing a secure communication channel between clients and servers?',
  'mcq',
  '["TLS (Transport Layer Security)","AES-256 (Advanced Encryption Standard)","SHA-256 (Secure Hash Algorithm)","MFA (Multi-Factor Authentication)"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is TLS (Transport Layer Security). TLS is a cryptographic protocol designed to provide communications security over a computer network. It is widely used for encrypting data in transit for web applications (HTTPS) and other network communications. Option B (incorrect) is AES-256, which is a symmetric encryption algorithm used for encrypting data at rest, not specifically for data in transit over a network. Option C (incorrect) is SHA-256, which is a cryptographic hash function used for data integrity and digital signatures, not for encryption of data in transit. Option D (incorrect) is MFA (Multi-Factor Authentication), which is an authentication method requiring multiple verification factors, not an encryption technology.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_69',
  'A Solutions Architect is designing a data archiving solution for regulatory compliance that requires data to be retained for seven years and remain immutable. Which Amazon S3 feature, combined with versioning, ensures this immutability?',
  'mcq',
  '["S3 Object Lock","S3 Glacier Deep Archive","S3 Lifecycle Policies","S3 Intelligent-Tiering"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is S3 Object Lock. S3 Object Lock provides WORM (Write Once Read Many) protection for objects, ensuring they cannot be overwritten or deleted for a fixed amount of time or indefinitely. This is crucial for regulatory compliance requiring data immutability. S3 Object Lock requires S3 Versioning to be enabled on the bucket. Option B (incorrect) is S3 Glacier Deep Archive, which is a storage class for very long-term, low-cost data archiving, but it does not inherently provide immutability; it''s a storage tier. Option C (incorrect) is S3 Lifecycle Policies, which automate the transition of objects between storage classes or their expiration after a certain period. While useful for cost management and retention, it doesn''t enforce immutability. Option D (incorrect) is S3 Intelligent-Tiering, which automatically moves objects between access tiers based on access patterns, optimizing storage costs. It does not provide immutability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_70',
  'Which AWS service provides dedicated hardware security modules (HSMs) in the AWS Cloud, offering FIPS 140-2 Level 3 compliance and full control over your encryption keys?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudHSM","AWS Key Management Service (KMS)","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS CloudHSM. AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated hardware security modules (HSMs) within the AWS Cloud. It offers FIPS 140-2 Level 3 compliance, which is a higher level than KMS, and gives customers exclusive control over their encryption keys and cryptographic operations. Option B (incorrect) is AWS Key Management Service (KMS), which is a managed service for creating and managing cryptographic keys, offering FIPS 140-2 Level 2 compliance. While highly secure, it doesn''t provide the same level of direct control over the HSM as CloudHSM. Option C (incorrect) is AWS Secrets Manager, which manages and rotates secrets, not dedicated HSMs for key management. Option D (incorrect) is AWS Certificate Manager (ACM), which manages SSL/TLS certificates, not general cryptographic keys or HSMs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_71',
  'A company needs to implement a robust backup strategy for their Amazon RDS databases, ensuring point-in-time recovery and automated backups. Which RDS feature should be enabled?',
  'mcq',
  '["Automated Backups","Manual Snapshots","Multi-AZ Deployment","Read Replicas"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Automated Backups. Amazon RDS Automated Backups enable point-in-time recovery for your DB instances. RDS automatically creates a storage volume snapshot of your DB instance and backs up transaction logs, allowing you to restore your DB instance to any second within your retention period. Option B (incorrect) is Manual Snapshots, which are user-initiated backups. While useful, they are not automated and do not provide point-in-time recovery capabilities like automated backups. Option C (incorrect) is Multi-AZ Deployment, which provides high availability and automatic failover to a standby instance in a different Availability Zone, but it is not primarily a backup and recovery solution. Option D (incorrect) is Read Replicas, which improve read performance and offload read traffic from the primary DB instance, but they are not a backup solution for the primary instance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_72',
  'What is the primary benefit of enabling S3 Versioning on an Amazon S3 bucket?',
  'mcq',
  '["To protect against accidental deletions and overwrites of objects","To automatically transition objects to lower-cost storage classes","To encrypt objects at rest with AWS-managed keys","To replicate objects to another AWS Region for disaster recovery"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the primary benefit of S3 Versioning. It keeps multiple versions of an object in the same bucket, allowing you to retrieve previous versions of objects that have been accidentally deleted or overwritten. Option B (incorrect) describes the function of S3 Lifecycle Policies, not versioning. Option C (incorrect) describes Server-Side Encryption with S3-managed keys (SSE-S3), which is a separate encryption feature. Option D (incorrect) describes S3 Cross-Region Replication, which is a separate feature for disaster recovery.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_73',
  'A Solutions Architect needs to classify and protect sensitive data stored in Amazon S3 buckets. Which AWS service uses machine learning to discover, classify, and protect sensitive data?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Macie","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Security Hub","Amazon Inspector"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon Macie. Amazon Macie is a data security and data privacy service that uses machine learning and pattern matching to discover, classify, and protect sensitive data in AWS, primarily in Amazon S3. It helps identify personally identifiable information (PII) and other sensitive data. Option B (incorrect) is Amazon GuardDuty, which is a threat detection service that monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior. Option C (incorrect) is AWS Security Hub, which provides a comprehensive view of your security alerts and security posture across your AWS accounts. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon Inspector, which is an automated security assessment service that helps identify vulnerabilities in EC2 instances and container images.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_74',
  'Which AWS service is used to record and continuously monitor API calls and configuration changes for your AWS account, providing a history of events for security analysis and compliance auditing?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail","AWS Config","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Security Hub"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS CloudTrail. AWS CloudTrail provides a record of actions taken by a user, role, or an AWS service in AWS. It records API calls and related events, allowing you to monitor and audit activity across your AWS infrastructure for security analysis, change tracking, and compliance auditing. Option B (incorrect) is AWS Config, which enables you to assess, audit, and evaluate the configurations of your AWS resources. It helps with compliance by tracking resource configuration changes over time, but it doesn''t primarily record API calls. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon CloudWatch, which is a monitoring and observability service that provides data and actionable insights to monitor applications, respond to system-wide performance changes, and optimize resource utilization. It doesn''t record API calls for auditing. Option D (incorrect) is AWS Security Hub, which provides a comprehensive view of your security alerts and security posture across your AWS accounts by aggregating findings from other AWS security services, but it doesn''t generate the audit logs itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_75',
  'A company needs to ensure that all data uploaded to an Amazon S3 bucket is encrypted at rest by default. Which S3 bucket setting should be configured?',
  'mcq',
  '["Default encryption for the S3 bucket","S3 Object Lock","S3 Versioning","S3 Transfer Acceleration"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Default encryption for the S3 bucket. By enabling default encryption, all new objects uploaded to the bucket are automatically encrypted at rest using either SSE-S3 or SSE-KMS, without requiring the client to specify encryption headers. Option B (incorrect) is S3 Object Lock, which provides WORM (Write Once Read Many) capabilities for immutability, not encryption. Option C (incorrect) is S3 Versioning, which keeps multiple versions of an object, protecting against accidental deletions or overwrites, but it does not provide encryption. Option D (incorrect) is S3 Transfer Acceleration, which speeds up long-distance data transfers to and from S3 buckets, but it is not related to encryption at rest.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_76',
  'A healthcare organization is storing patient health information (PHI) in an Amazon S3 bucket. Due to strict compliance regulations (e.g., HIPAA), all data must be encrypted at rest using customer-managed keys, and the organization must have full control over the encryption keys, including the ability to audit key usage and rotate them on demand. The solution must also integrate seamlessly with Amazon S3. Which encryption option should a Solutions Architect recommend for the Amazon S3 bucket to meet these stringent compliance requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS (SSE-KMS) using a customer-managed key (CMK) in AWS KMS.","Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3), leveraging AWS-managed encryption keys.","Client-Side Encryption (CSE) with a client-side master key managed by the application before uploading to Amazon S3.","Server-Side Encryption with Customer-Provided Keys (SSE-C), where the customer provides the encryption key with each request."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most appropriate solution. Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS (SSE-KMS) allows the use of customer-managed keys (CMKs) in AWS KMS. This provides the organization with full control over the encryption keys, including the ability to define key policies, audit key usage through AWS CloudTrail, and rotate keys on demand, which are critical requirements for HIPAA compliance and PHI. Option B is incorrect because Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) uses AWS-managed keys, meaning the customer does not have control over the key rotation or the ability to audit key usage, which violates the compliance requirement for customer-managed keys and audit trails. This represents anti-pattern [ap-1.3-001]. Option C is incorrect because Client-Side Encryption (CSE) requires the application to manage the encryption and decryption process, including the master key. While it provides full control, it significantly increases the operational overhead and complexity for the application developers, which might not be desirable for a seamless integration. Option D is incorrect because Server-Side Encryption with Customer-Provided Keys (SSE-C) requires the customer to provide the encryption key with every request. While it gives control, it does not integrate with AWS KMS for key management, rotation, or auditing, and places the burden of key management and secure transmission on the client, which is less robust than SSE-KMS for this scenario.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_77',
  'A media company stores large volumes of video archives in Amazon S3. Due to legal and regulatory requirements, some of these archives must be retained for a minimum of seven years and remain immutable, meaning they cannot be deleted or overwritten for the specified retention period, even by root users. The company needs a solution that prevents accidental or malicious deletion and ensures compliance with these immutability requirements. Which Amazon S3 feature should a Solutions Architect recommend to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3 Object Lock configured in Compliance mode for the required retention period.","Amazon S3 Lifecycle policies to transition objects to Amazon S3 Glacier after seven years, preventing deletion.","Amazon S3 Versioning enabled on the bucket, combined with a bucket policy that denies `s3:DeleteObject` actions.","AWS Backup service to create immutable backups of the S3 bucket, separate from the primary S3 storage."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective solution. Amazon S3 Object Lock, specifically in Compliance mode, prevents an object from being overwritten or deleted by any user, including the root user, until a predefined retention period expires. This directly addresses the immutability requirement for legal and regulatory compliance. It also requires S3 Versioning to be enabled on the bucket. Option B is incorrect because Amazon S3 Lifecycle policies are used for cost optimization by transitioning objects to different storage classes or expiring them, but they do not provide immutability or prevent deletion during the retention period in the same way Object Lock does. Option C is incorrect because while Amazon S3 Versioning helps recover from accidental deletions by keeping previous versions, and a bucket policy can deny `s3:DeleteObject` actions, a determined or malicious actor with sufficient permissions (e.g., a root user or an IAM user with `s3:BypassGovernanceRetention` if Governance mode is used) could potentially modify the policy or delete versions. Object Lock''s Compliance mode offers stronger immutability. Option D is incorrect because while AWS Backup can create backups, the primary requirement is to make the objects in the *original* S3 bucket immutable and undeletable for a specific period, not just to have separate backups. AWS Backup provides recovery, but not the in-place immutability for compliance on the primary data store.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_78',
  'A software company is developing a new SaaS application that will encrypt all customer data at rest. The company''s security policy dictates that encryption keys must be generated and stored in a FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated hardware security module (HSM) and that the company must have exclusive control over the cryptographic operations and key management lifecycle. The solution needs to be highly available and scalable across multiple Availability Zones. Which AWS service should a Solutions Architect recommend for managing these encryption keys?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudHSM, providing dedicated, FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated HSM instances under the customer''s exclusive control.","AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS), utilizing customer-managed keys (CMKs) for encryption.","AWS Secrets Manager, to store the encryption keys as secrets and retrieve them as needed.","An Amazon EC2 instance running a third-party software-based key management solution."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most appropriate solution. AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated hardware security modules (HSMs) that are FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated. It gives the customer exclusive control over the cryptographic operations and the key management lifecycle, directly meeting the stringent security and compliance requirements for FIPS 140-2 Level 3 and exclusive key control. CloudHSM clusters can be deployed across multiple Availability Zones for high availability and scalability. Option B is incorrect because while AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) uses FIPS 140-2 Level 2 validated hardware security modules and offers customer-managed keys (CMKs), it is a multi-tenant service where AWS manages the underlying HSMs. It does not provide the customer with exclusive control over the HSMs or the FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validation with exclusive control required by the scenario. This represents anti-pattern [ap-1.3-004]. Option C is incorrect because AWS Secrets Manager is designed for storing and rotating application secrets like database credentials and API keys, not for managing the cryptographic keys themselves within a FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated HSM with exclusive control. Option D is incorrect because running a third-party software-based key management solution on an Amazon EC2 instance would likely not meet the FIPS 140-2 Level 3 hardware validation requirement, would introduce significant operational overhead for management and scaling, and would not provide the same level of dedicated hardware security as CloudHSM.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_79',
  'A global enterprise requires a highly secure and compliant solution for storing and processing sensitive customer data. The data must be encrypted at rest and in transit, and access to the data must be strictly controlled based on the principle of least privilege. The company also needs to ensure that all access attempts and modifications to the data are logged for auditing purposes. The solution must be scalable and maintainable with minimal administrative effort. Which combination of AWS services and practices should a Solutions Architect recommend?',
  'mcq',
  '["Store data in Amazon S3 with SSE-KMS, enforce access with IAM policies and S3 bucket policies, and enable AWS CloudTrail for logging.","Store data in Amazon EBS volumes attached to Amazon EC2 instances, encrypt volumes with customer-managed keys, and use Security Groups for access control.","Store data in Amazon RDS with encryption at rest, use database user accounts for access control, and rely on database logs for auditing.","Store data in Amazon DynamoDB with encryption at rest, use Amazon Cognito for user authentication, and enable Amazon CloudWatch for monitoring."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) provides a robust and scalable solution for storing sensitive data with strong security controls and auditing. Amazon S3 with Server-Side Encryption using AWS KMS (SSE-KMS) ensures data is encrypted at rest with customer-managed keys, providing control and auditability. IAM policies and S3 bucket policies enforce the principle of least privilege for data access. AWS CloudTrail provides comprehensive logging of all API calls and actions on AWS resources, enabling detailed auditing. This combination meets all specified requirements with relatively low administrative effort. Option B is incorrect because while Amazon EBS encryption and Security Groups provide security, managing data on EC2 instances requires more operational overhead for patching, scaling, and backup compared to managed services. Also, Security Groups are allow-only and less granular for data access control compared to IAM and S3 bucket policies. Option C is incorrect because while Amazon RDS provides encryption at rest and database logs, relying solely on database user accounts for access control can be less integrated with AWS''s centralized identity management (IAM) compared to S3. Auditing database logs can also be more complex than CloudTrail for overall AWS resource access. Option D is incorrect because while Amazon DynamoDB offers encryption at rest, Amazon Cognito is primarily for user authentication in web/mobile applications, not for fine-grained authorization to DynamoDB tables based on least privilege for backend services. Amazon CloudWatch is for monitoring, not for comprehensive auditing of data access and modifications to meet compliance requirements; AWS CloudTrail is specifically designed for that.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_80',
  'A company is migrating a critical on-premises application to AWS. The application requires highly available and low-latency access to a shared file system that must be accessible by multiple Amazon EC2 instances concurrently. The security team mandates that all data at rest on the file system must be encrypted using customer-managed keys (CMKs) from AWS KMS, and all access to the file system must be controlled through IAM. The solution needs to minimize operational overhead for file system management. Which AWS service should a Solutions Architect recommend?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) with encryption at rest using AWS KMS CMKs and IAM access control.","Amazon FSx for Windows File Server with encryption at rest and Active Directory integration for access control.","Amazon S3 with S3 File Gateway to provide file interface, using SSE-KMS for encryption and IAM for access.","An Amazon EC2 instance configured as a Network File System (NFS) server, with encrypted EBS volumes and IAM roles."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most suitable solution. Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) provides a simple, scalable, elastic, and highly available file system for Amazon EC2 instances. EFS supports encryption at rest using AWS KMS customer-managed keys (CMKs) and allows access control through IAM policies, meeting all the specified security and operational requirements. It minimizes operational overhead as it''s a fully managed service. Option B is incorrect because Amazon FSx for Windows File Server is designed for Windows-based applications and requires Active Directory for access control, which is not specified as a requirement and would add complexity if not already in use. While it supports encryption, EFS is generally more appropriate for Linux-based EC2 instances and simpler IAM integration for a shared file system. Option C is incorrect because Amazon S3 with S3 File Gateway provides a file interface to S3 object storage. While S3 supports SSE-KMS and IAM, it''s an object storage service, and the File Gateway adds a layer of complexity and potential performance overhead that might not be ideal for a direct, low-latency shared file system for EC2 instances compared to EFS. Option D is incorrect because configuring an Amazon EC2 instance as an NFS server introduces significant operational overhead for managing, patching, scaling, and ensuring high availability of the NFS server itself, which contradicts the requirement for minimal operational overhead.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_81',
  'A company is building a new application that needs to store highly sensitive customer data in an Amazon S3 bucket. The data must be encrypted at rest, and the encryption keys must be managed by the customer, with the ability to audit all key usage. The company also requires that the data be protected against accidental deletion or modification for a fixed period to comply with regulatory requirements. Which combination of Amazon S3 features and AWS services should a Solutions Architect recommend?',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable Amazon S3 Versioning and S3 Object Lock in Compliance mode, and configure Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS (SSE-KMS) using a customer-managed key (CMK).","Enable Amazon S3 Versioning and configure an S3 Lifecycle policy to move objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, and use Server-Side Encryption with S3-managed keys (SSE-S3).","Configure Server-Side Encryption with Customer-Provided Keys (SSE-C) and implement a custom application logic to prevent object deletion for a fixed period.","Use Amazon S3 with default encryption enabled, and create an IAM policy that explicitly denies `s3:DeleteObject` and `s3:PutObject` for all users."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most comprehensive solution. Enabling Amazon S3 Versioning is a prerequisite for S3 Object Lock and helps recover from accidental overwrites. S3 Object Lock in Compliance mode provides strong immutability, preventing deletion or modification for a fixed period, even by the root user, meeting regulatory requirements. Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS (SSE-KMS) using a customer-managed key (CMK) ensures data is encrypted at rest, and the customer has full control over the key, including auditing key usage via AWS CloudTrail. Option B is incorrect because S3 Lifecycle policies are for cost optimization and data tiering, not for enforcing immutability against deletion or modification. SSE-S3 uses AWS-managed keys, which does not give the customer control or auditability over the keys, violating a key requirement ([ap-1.3-001]). Option C is incorrect because while SSE-C provides customer-controlled encryption, it requires the customer to manage and provide the key with every request, increasing operational complexity. Implementing custom application logic for deletion prevention is prone to errors and less robust than S3 Object Lock. Option D is incorrect because while default encryption is good, it typically uses SSE-S3 or SSE-KMS (if configured), but an IAM policy denying `s3:DeleteObject` and `s3:PutObject` can be circumvented by users with sufficient permissions (e.g., root user or IAM users with `iam:PassRole` and `s3:BypassGovernanceRetention` for Object Lock). It does not provide the same level of immutable protection as S3 Object Lock in Compliance mode.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_82',
  'A company is designing a new application that will process and store highly sensitive financial transaction data. The security team mandates that all data must be encrypted at rest and in transit. Furthermore, the encryption keys used for data at rest must be customer-managed, with a strict requirement for regular, automated key rotation and the ability to audit all key usage. The solution must be highly available and integrated with other AWS services. Which AWS service should a Solutions Architect recommend for managing these encryption keys?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) with customer-managed keys (CMKs) configured for automatic key rotation.","AWS CloudHSM, providing dedicated hardware security modules for key generation and storage.","AWS Secrets Manager, storing the encryption keys as secrets and managing their rotation.","Amazon S3, storing the encryption keys in a highly secure, encrypted bucket."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most appropriate solution. AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) allows you to create and manage customer-managed keys (CMKs). KMS CMKs support automatic key rotation, which is a critical requirement. All key usage is logged to AWS CloudTrail, providing a clear audit trail. KMS is highly available, integrated with many AWS services, and provides a managed solution for encryption key management, meeting all the specified requirements. Option B is incorrect because while AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated hardware security modules and FIPS 140-2 Level 3 compliance, it requires significant operational overhead for management and scaling of the HSMs themselves. For most use cases requiring customer-managed keys and automatic rotation, KMS offers a more managed and less operationally intensive solution, unless FIPS 140-2 Level 3 with exclusive control is an absolute must. This represents anti-pattern [ap-1.3-004] if KMS is sufficient. Option C is incorrect because AWS Secrets Manager is designed for managing application secrets like database credentials and API keys, not for managing the cryptographic keys used for data encryption at rest in services like S3 or RDS. While it can store secrets, it''s not a KMS replacement. Option D is incorrect because storing encryption keys directly in Amazon S3, even in an encrypted bucket, is not a secure or recommended practice for key management. S3 is object storage, not a dedicated key management service, and lacks features like automatic rotation, secure key generation, and direct integration with other services for encryption operations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  84,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_83',
  'A company is migrating a legacy application to AWS. The application requires access to a shared file system that supports the SMB protocol and needs to be integrated with an existing on-premises Microsoft Active Directory for user authentication and access control. The solution must be fully managed, highly available, and provide encryption of data at rest and in transit. Which AWS service should a Solutions Architect recommend to meet these requirements with minimal operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon FSx for Windows File Server, integrated with AWS Directory Service for Microsoft Active Directory.","Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS), configured with an EFS Access Point for SMB access.","An Amazon EC2 instance running Windows Server with a shared folder, joined to the Active Directory.","Amazon S3 with S3 File Gateway, configured for SMB access and integrated with Active Directory."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most appropriate solution. Amazon FSx for Windows File Server provides a fully managed, highly available, and scalable native Windows file system that supports the SMB protocol. It seamlessly integrates with AWS Directory Service for Microsoft Active Directory (which can be connected to an on-premises AD), allowing for centralized user authentication and access control. It also supports encryption of data at rest and in transit, meeting all requirements with minimal operational overhead. Option B is incorrect because Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) is primarily designed for Linux-based workloads and supports NFS protocol, not native SMB. While it can be accessed from Windows, it''s not a native SMB file system and does not integrate directly with Active Directory for user authentication in the same way FSx for Windows File Server does. EFS Access Points are for application-specific access, not for SMB. Option C is incorrect because deploying and managing an Amazon EC2 instance as a Windows Server with a shared folder introduces significant operational overhead for patching, scaling, high availability, and backup, which contradicts the requirement for a fully managed solution with minimal overhead. Option D is incorrect because Amazon S3 with S3 File Gateway provides an SMB interface to S3 object storage. While it can integrate with Active Directory, it''s an object storage solution presented as a file share, which might not offer the same performance or native Windows file system features as FSx for Windows File Server, especially for applications expecting a true file system.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  85,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_84',
  'A healthcare organization needs to store patient records in Amazon S3 with an immutable retention policy for seven years to meet regulatory compliance. The solution must prevent accidental or malicious deletion and modification of objects during this period. Which Amazon S3 feature, when enabled on the bucket, will satisfy this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable S3 Versioning on the bucket and apply an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects to S3 Glacier.","Enable S3 Versioning on the bucket and configure S3 Object Lock in WORM (Write Once Read Many) mode.","Configure S3 Cross-Region Replication to another bucket with a different AWS account.","Apply an AWS IAM bucket policy that explicitly denies `s3:DeleteObject` actions for all users."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the appropriate solution. S3 Object Lock, when configured in WORM (Write Once Read Many) mode, prevents objects from being deleted or overwritten for a fixed amount of time or indefinitely, meeting immutable retention requirements for compliance. S3 Versioning must be enabled on the bucket before Object Lock can be applied. Option A (incorrect) involves S3 Versioning and Lifecycle policies. While versioning protects against accidental overwrites and lifecycle policies can manage object transitions, they do not provide the immutable WORM protection required to prevent deletion or modification for compliance. Option C (incorrect) is S3 Cross-Region Replication, which creates copies of objects in another region, enhancing durability and disaster recovery, but it does not inherently provide immutability or prevent deletion/modification of the original or replicated objects. Option D (incorrect) is an AWS IAM bucket policy that can restrict delete actions for users, but it does not provide true immutability. A highly privileged user or the root user could still modify or remove the policy, or modify existing objects, bypassing the intended protection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  86,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

