INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_63',
  'What is the primary purpose of Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling?',
  'mcq',
  '["To automatically adjust the number of EC2 instances in response to demand","To provision and manage virtual private clouds (VPCs)","To encrypt data at rest on EBS volumes","To distribute incoming application traffic across multiple targets"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (To automatically adjust the number of EC2 instances in response to demand) is correct. Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling automatically adds or removes EC2 instances based on defined policies, schedules, and health checks, ensuring that applications have the right amount of compute capacity. Option B describes the function of Amazon VPC. Option C describes a feature of Amazon EBS. Option D describes the function of Elastic Load Balancing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_64',
  'Which AWS service is best suited for running long-running, fault-tolerant batch processing jobs that can tolerate interruptions to save costs?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2 Spot Instances","AWS Lambda","Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances","Amazon Fargate"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon EC2 Spot Instances) is the most cost-effective solution for fault-tolerant batch processing jobs that can handle interruptions. Spot Instances offer significant savings (up to 90% off On-Demand prices) in exchange for the possibility of interruption when EC2 needs the capacity back. Option B (AWS Lambda) is incorrect because it has a maximum execution duration of 15 minutes, making it unsuitable for long-running jobs. Option C (Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances) provides consistent capacity but at a higher cost, making it less cost-effective for fault-tolerant batch jobs. Option D (Amazon Fargate) provides serverless container compute but does not offer the same cost savings for interruptible workloads as Spot Instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_65',
  'A company needs to run a highly scalable web application that requires dynamic scaling of compute resources based on user traffic. Which AWS service combination provides this elasticity and high performance?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2 with EC2 Auto Scaling and an Application Load Balancer","AWS Lambda with Amazon SQS","Amazon EMR with Amazon S3","AWS Batch with Amazon DynamoDB"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon EC2 with EC2 Auto Scaling and an Application Load Balancer) is the correct combination for a highly scalable web application requiring dynamic scaling. The Application Load Balancer distributes incoming traffic, and EC2 Auto Scaling automatically adjusts the number of EC2 instances to match demand, providing elasticity and high performance. Option B (AWS Lambda with Amazon SQS) is suitable for event-driven, serverless architectures, but for a traditional web application requiring persistent compute, EC2 with Auto Scaling is more appropriate. Option C (Amazon EMR with Amazon S3) is designed for big data processing, not a web application. Option D (AWS Batch with Amazon DynamoDB) is for batch computing workloads and a NoSQL database, not typically for a general-purpose scalable web application.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_66',
  'What is the maximum execution duration for a single AWS Lambda function invocation?',
  'mcq',
  '["15 minutes","5 minutes","30 minutes","1 hour"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (15 minutes) is the correct maximum execution duration for an AWS Lambda function. This limit is a hard constraint, and functions exceeding this time will be terminated. Option B (5 minutes) was an older limit but has since been increased. Options C and D are incorrect as Lambda functions cannot run for 30 minutes or 1 hour.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_67',
  'A development team wants to deploy containerized applications without managing the underlying servers. They need a serverless compute engine for their Amazon ECS clusters. Which AWS service should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Fargate","Amazon EC2","AWS Lambda","AWS Batch"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon Fargate) is the correct choice. Amazon Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers that works with both Amazon ECS and Amazon EKS, allowing users to run containers without provisioning or managing servers. Option B (Amazon EC2) requires managing the underlying virtual machines. Option C (AWS Lambda) is for serverless functions, not containers, and has a 15-minute execution limit. Option D (AWS Batch) is for running batch computing workloads, which can use Fargate, but Fargate itself is the serverless compute engine for general container deployment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_68',
  'Which AWS service allows you to run containers on EC2 instances that you manage, providing granular control over the infrastructure?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon ECS on EC2 launch type","Amazon Fargate","AWS Lambda","AWS Batch"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon ECS on EC2 launch type) is the correct choice. This launch type allows you to run your containerized applications on a cluster of Amazon EC2 instances that you provision and manage, giving you granular control over the server infrastructure. Option B (Amazon Fargate) is a serverless compute engine where AWS manages the underlying infrastructure. Option C (AWS Lambda) is for serverless functions, not containers on managed EC2 instances. Option D (AWS Batch) is for batch computing workloads, which can use EC2, but ECS on EC2 is the direct answer for running containers with managed EC2 instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_69',
  'A data processing application needs to execute a series of tasks, some of which can run for several hours. The team wants to use a serverless approach where possible and manage the workflow. Which service should they use for orchestrating these long-running tasks?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Step Functions","AWS Lambda","Amazon SQS","Amazon Fargate"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Step Functions) is the correct choice for orchestrating long-running, multi-step workflows, including tasks that might exceed Lambda''s 15-minute limit. Step Functions can coordinate various AWS services, including Lambda functions and Fargate tasks, to build complex workflows. Option B (AWS Lambda) is incorrect because individual Lambda functions have a maximum execution time of 15 minutes, making them unsuitable for tasks that run for several hours. Option C (Amazon SQS) is a message queuing service, not an orchestration service. Option D (Amazon Fargate) can run long-running containerized tasks but does not provide the workflow orchestration capabilities needed to manage a series of tasks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_70',
  'Which AWS compute service is ideal for highly parallel, large-scale scientific or engineering workloads that can be broken down into independent tasks?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Batch","Amazon EC2","AWS Lambda","Amazon Fargate"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Batch) is the correct choice. AWS Batch enables developers, scientists, and engineers to easily and efficiently run hundreds of thousands of batch computing jobs on AWS. It dynamically provisions the optimal quantity and type of compute resources (e.g., EC2 instances, Spot Instances, Fargate) based on the volume and resource requirements of the batch jobs. Option B (Amazon EC2) provides virtual servers but requires manual management for batch job orchestration. Option C (AWS Lambda) is for short-lived, event-driven functions. Option D (Amazon Fargate) is for running serverless containers, but AWS Batch provides the orchestration layer specifically for batch workloads.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_71',
  'A company is running a web application on Amazon EC2 instances and wants to ensure high availability and automatically recover from instance failures. Which AWS service should be configured to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Systems Manager","AWS CloudFormation"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling) is the correct choice. EC2 Auto Scaling can automatically replace unhealthy instances, ensuring that the application maintains its desired capacity and availability. It monitors the health of instances and launches new ones if an existing instance becomes unhealthy or terminates. Option B (Amazon CloudWatch) is a monitoring service that can trigger Auto Scaling actions but doesn''t perform the recovery itself. Option C (AWS Systems Manager) is for operational insights and management of EC2 instances, not automatic recovery from failures. Option D (AWS CloudFormation) is an infrastructure as code service for provisioning resources, not for dynamic instance recovery.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_72',
  'What is the primary purpose of AWS Lambda?',
  'mcq',
  '["To run code without provisioning or managing servers","To store objects in the cloud","To manage relational databases","To distribute network traffic"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (To run code without provisioning or managing servers) is the primary purpose of AWS Lambda. It is a serverless compute service that executes code in response to events, automatically managing the underlying compute resources. Option B (To store objects in the cloud) is the purpose of Amazon S3. Option C (To manage relational databases) is the purpose of Amazon RDS. Option D (To distribute network traffic) is the purpose of services like Elastic Load Balancing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_73',
  'Which AWS service enables you to run containerized applications on a fully managed, serverless compute engine, eliminating the need to provision or manage servers?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Fargate","Amazon EC2","AWS Lambda","AWS Batch"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon Fargate) is the correct choice. Amazon Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers that works with Amazon ECS and Amazon EKS, allowing you to run containers without managing the underlying EC2 instances. Option B (Amazon EC2) requires manual server management. Option C (AWS Lambda) is for serverless functions, not containers. Option D (AWS Batch) is for orchestrating batch jobs, which can use Fargate, but Fargate itself is the serverless container runtime.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_74',
  'A global e-commerce platform experiences highly variable traffic, with peak loads during flash sales and holidays causing significant spikes in compute demand. The platform''s backend is built on a microservices architecture using Docker containers. The operations team aims to minimize infrastructure management overhead and ensure applications scale automatically to handle sudden traffic surges while optimizing costs. The solution must maintain high availability across multiple Availability Zones.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy containers on Amazon ECS with EC2 launch type, using EC2 Auto Scaling groups configured with target tracking policies for scaling.","Deploy containers on Amazon ECS with AWS Fargate launch type, leveraging Fargate''s serverless operational model and automatic scaling.","Deploy containers on Amazon EKS with EC2 worker nodes, using Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler and Cluster Autoscaler.","Provision a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances with Docker installed, and use AWS Auto Scaling to adjust capacity based on CPU utilization."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) best meets the requirements for minimizing infrastructure management overhead and automatic scaling for containerized applications. AWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers that removes the need to provision, configure, and scale clusters of virtual machines. It automatically scales compute capacity to match demand, aligning perfectly with variable traffic patterns and minimizing operational burden. Option A uses ECS with EC2 launch type, which still requires managing and scaling the underlying EC2 instances, increasing operational overhead compared to Fargate. Option C, Amazon EKS with EC2 worker nodes, also requires managing the Kubernetes control plane (though AWS manages it for EKS) and the EC2 worker nodes, which is more complex and requires more management than Fargate. Option D involves manually provisioning EC2 instances and managing Docker, which is a highly operational approach and does not leverage the benefits of container orchestration or serverless compute for containers.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_75',
  'A company runs a critical batch processing application that takes approximately 30 minutes to process each job. The application is fault-tolerant, meaning individual job failures can be retried without impacting the overall outcome. The company needs to process thousands of these jobs daily, with varying concurrency requirements, and is looking for the most cost-effective solution that minimizes operational overhead.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the batch processing application on AWS Lambda functions, triggering them for each job.","Provision a fleet of Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances, and manage job orchestration with a custom script.","Utilize AWS Batch, configured to use Amazon EC2 Spot Instances for its compute environment.","Run the batch processing application on Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances to ensure consistent capacity."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most cost-effective and operationally efficient solution. AWS Batch is a fully managed service that plans, schedules, and executes batch computing workloads across a range of AWS compute services. By configuring AWS Batch to use Amazon EC2 Spot Instances, the company can achieve significant cost savings (60-90% compared to On-Demand) for its fault-tolerant batch jobs, which is a key anti-pattern (ap-3.2-003) if not utilized. AWS Batch handles the complexities of instance provisioning, scaling, and job management. Option A, using AWS Lambda, is unsuitable because Lambda functions have a maximum execution timeout of 15 minutes, making them inappropriate for jobs lasting 30 minutes (ap-3.2-001). Option B, provisioning EC2 On-Demand Instances with custom orchestration, would incur higher costs and significant operational overhead for managing the infrastructure and job scheduling. Option D, using EC2 Reserved Instances, provides cost savings for predictable, long-running workloads but is less flexible and cost-effective for variable batch processing compared to Spot Instances, especially when fault tolerance allows for interruptions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_76',
  'A company is migrating a legacy application that runs on a single, large on-premises server. The application is critical and requires high availability, but its workload is mostly idle, with occasional bursts of activity during month-end reporting. The company wants to move this application to AWS, ensuring high availability and elasticity, while minimizing costs for the idle periods and avoiding over-provisioning.',
  'mcq',
  '["Provision a large Amazon EC2 On-Demand instance to match the legacy server''s capacity.","Deploy the application on Amazon EC2 instances within an Auto Scaling group configured to scale based on CPU utilization.","Migrate the application to AWS Lambda functions to leverage serverless computing.","Use Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances for a fixed capacity, ensuring consistent performance."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate solution. Deploying the application on Amazon EC2 instances within an Auto Scaling group, configured to scale based on metrics like CPU utilization, ensures high availability by distributing instances across Availability Zones and automatically replaces unhealthy instances. More importantly, it provides elasticity by scaling out during bursts and scaling in during idle periods, preventing over-provisioning and minimizing costs (ap-3.2-002). Option A, provisioning a single large On-Demand instance, does not provide high availability and would incur costs even during idle periods, leading to over-provisioning. Option C, migrating to AWS Lambda, might require significant re-architecture if the application is not designed for serverless event-driven patterns, and Lambda has execution limits (ap-3.2-001) that might not suit a legacy application. Option D, using EC2 Reserved Instances, provides cost savings for consistent, predictable workloads but does not address the elasticity requirement for variable workloads or the need to avoid over-provisioning during idle times.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_77',
  'A company is developing a new microservices application using Docker containers. The development team wants to deploy these containers to AWS with minimal operational overhead, focusing solely on application code and container images, without managing the underlying EC2 instances or cluster infrastructure. The application needs to scale automatically based on demand and integrate with other AWS services.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy containers on Amazon ECS using the EC2 launch type.","Run containers on Amazon EC2 instances with Docker Swarm.","Utilize Amazon ECS with the AWS Fargate launch type.","Set up an Amazon EKS cluster with self-managed EC2 worker nodes."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the best solution for deploying containers with minimal operational overhead. Amazon ECS with the AWS Fargate launch type provides a serverless compute engine for containers, meaning you don''t need to provision, configure, or scale clusters of virtual machines. You only pay for the compute resources consumed by your containers, and Fargate handles all the underlying infrastructure management, perfectly aligning with the ''zero infrastructure management'' requirement (ap-3.2-004). Option A, ECS with EC2 launch type, requires managing the underlying EC2 instances, including patching, scaling, and capacity planning. Option B, running Docker Swarm on EC2, is a self-managed solution that incurs significant operational overhead. Option D, Amazon EKS with self-managed EC2 worker nodes, also requires managing the underlying EC2 instances and the Kubernetes cluster itself, which is more complex than Fargate.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_78',
  'A development team needs to deploy containerized microservices without managing any underlying servers or clusters. The services are stateless and can scale independently. Which AWS compute solution offers the LEAST operational overhead for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Fargate","Amazon EC2 with Auto Scaling","Amazon ECS on EC2","AWS Batch"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Fargate) is the correct choice because it is a serverless compute engine for containers, allowing the team to run containers without having to provision, manage, or scale EC2 instances. This provides the least operational overhead. Option B (Amazon EC2 with Auto Scaling) requires managing the underlying EC2 instances, including patching, security, and operating system maintenance, which increases operational overhead. Option C (Amazon ECS on EC2) also requires managing the EC2 instances that form the ECS cluster, similar to Option B. Option D (AWS Batch) is designed for running batch computing workloads and while it can use Fargate, its primary use case is not for continuous, stateless microservices, and it can also run on EC2 instances requiring management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

