INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_57',
  'What is the main advantage of AWS Global Accelerator over Amazon CloudFront for non-HTTP/HTTPS traffic?',
  'mcq',
  '["It supports caching of dynamic content at edge locations.","It uses the AWS global network to route TCP/UDP traffic.","It provides a dedicated private connection to AWS.","It automatically scales EC2 instances based on traffic."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the main advantage. AWS Global Accelerator improves the availability and performance of applications by routing traffic through the AWS global network at the network layer (Layer 3/4), making it suitable for both HTTP/HTTPS and non-HTTP/HTTPS (TCP/UDP) traffic. Option A describes a feature of Amazon CloudFront. Option C describes AWS Direct Connect. Option D describes Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_58',
  'A company wants to ensure consistent, low-latency connectivity between its corporate network and AWS resources, bypassing the public internet. Which service is appropriate?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS VPN","Amazon CloudFront","AWS Global Accelerator","AWS Direct Connect"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is AWS Direct Connect, which provides a dedicated network connection from your premises to AWS, ensuring consistent low-latency and high-throughput connectivity that bypasses the public internet. Option A (AWS VPN) uses the public internet. Option B (Amazon CloudFront) is a CDN. Option C (AWS Global Accelerator) routes traffic over the AWS global network but doesn''t provide a dedicated private connection from on-premises.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_59',
  'What is the primary benefit of using Amazon CloudFront for content delivery?',
  'mcq',
  '["Reduces latency for end-users by caching content at edge locations","Provides direct private network connectivity to AWS","Automates the deployment of web applications","Manages DNS records for domain names"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Reduces latency for end-users by caching content at edge locations) is the primary benefit of Amazon CloudFront. CloudFront is a content delivery network (CDN) service that securely delivers data, videos, applications, and APIs to customers globally with low latency and high transfer speeds by caching content closer to users. Option B describes AWS Direct Connect. Option C describes services like AWS Elastic Beanstalk or AWS CodeDeploy. Option D describes Amazon Route 53.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_60',
  'Which AWS service provides a dedicated, private network connection from your on-premises data center to AWS?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Direct Connect","AWS VPN","Amazon CloudFront","AWS Global Accelerator"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Direct Connect) is the correct service for establishing a dedicated, private network connection from an on-premises data center to AWS. This provides consistent network performance and reduced bandwidth costs compared to internet-based connections. Option B (AWS VPN) provides secure connections over the public internet, which can have variable latency. Option C (Amazon CloudFront) is a content delivery network. Option D (AWS Global Accelerator) improves application availability and performance using the AWS global network, but it doesn''t provide a dedicated private connection from on-premises.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_61',
  'A company needs to distribute incoming HTTP and HTTPS traffic across multiple EC2 instances in different Availability Zones. Which AWS service should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["Application Load Balancer (ALB)","Network Load Balancer (NLB)","Amazon Route 53","AWS Global Accelerator"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Application Load Balancer (ALB)) is the correct choice for distributing HTTP and HTTPS traffic. ALBs operate at Layer 7 (application layer) and provide advanced routing features based on content, host headers, and URL paths, making them ideal for web applications. Option B (Network Load Balancer (NLB)) operates at Layer 4 (transport layer) and is best for extreme performance, static IP addresses, and TCP/UDP traffic. Option C (Amazon Route 53) is a DNS service, not a load balancer. Option D (AWS Global Accelerator) improves global application performance but works at a higher level, directing traffic to the nearest regional endpoint, which would then typically use an ALB or NLB.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_62',
  'Which AWS service uses the AWS global network to improve the availability and performance of your applications by directing user traffic to the optimal endpoint?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Global Accelerator","Amazon CloudFront","AWS Direct Connect","Amazon Route 53"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Global Accelerator) is the correct service. AWS Global Accelerator improves the availability and performance of your applications with a single entry point that uses the AWS global network infrastructure to direct user traffic to the optimal application endpoint. Option B (Amazon CloudFront) is a CDN primarily for caching content at edge locations. Option C (AWS Direct Connect) provides private connectivity to AWS. Option D (Amazon Route 53) is a DNS service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_63',
  'A solutions architect needs to connect 15 VPCs in a hub-and-spoke topology to allow them to communicate with each other and with an on-premises network. Which networking service is the MOST scalable and manageable for this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Transit Gateway","VPC Peering","AWS VPN","AWS Direct Connect"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Transit Gateway) is the most scalable and manageable solution for connecting multiple VPCs (especially 10+) in a hub-and-spoke topology and also connecting to on-premises networks. It acts as a central hub for all network connections. Option B (VPC Peering) is non-transitive, meaning each VPC would need a direct peering connection to every other VPC, leading to an O(n^2) complexity for many VPCs, which is unmanageable for 15 VPCs. Option C (AWS VPN) provides secure connections but is typically used for point-to-point connections or for connecting a few VPCs to on-premises, not as a scalable hub for many VPCs. Option D (AWS Direct Connect) provides private connectivity to AWS but is not a service for interconnecting multiple VPCs within AWS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_64',
  'Which type of load balancer operates at the Layer 4 (transport layer) and is best suited for extreme performance and static IP addresses?',
  'mcq',
  '["Network Load Balancer (NLB)","Application Load Balancer (ALB)","Classic Load Balancer (CLB)","Gateway Load Balancer (GLB)"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Network Load Balancer (NLB)) is the correct choice. NLBs operate at Layer 4 (TCP, UDP, TLS) and are designed for extreme performance, low latency, and handling millions of requests per second. They also provide static IP addresses per Availability Zone. Option B (Application Load Balancer (ALB)) operates at Layer 7 (HTTP/HTTPS) and offers advanced routing features. Option C (Classic Load Balancer (CLB)) is a legacy load balancer. Option D (Gateway Load Balancer (GLB)) is used for deploying, scaling, and managing third-party virtual appliances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_65',
  'A company requires a highly available and secure connection to AWS over the public internet. Which AWS service should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS VPN","AWS Direct Connect","Amazon CloudFront","AWS Global Accelerator"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS VPN) is the correct service for establishing a secure and highly available connection to AWS over the public internet. It encrypts traffic between your on-premises network and your VPC. Option B (AWS Direct Connect) provides a dedicated private connection, not over the public internet. Option C (Amazon CloudFront) is a content delivery network. Option D (AWS Global Accelerator) improves performance and availability but doesn''t establish the secure tunnel itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_66',
  'What is the primary function of an Amazon VPC subnet?',
  'mcq',
  '["To divide a VPC into smaller, isolated network segments","To provide public internet access to EC2 instances","To encrypt data in transit between EC2 instances","To manage DNS resolution for a domain"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (To divide a VPC into smaller, isolated network segments) is the primary function of an Amazon VPC subnet. Subnets allow you to partition your VPC''s IP address range into smaller segments, which can then be associated with specific Availability Zones for high availability and used to isolate different tiers of an application (e.g., public and private subnets). Option B is achieved through an Internet Gateway and route tables, not solely by a subnet. Option C is handled by network security features like Security Groups and Network ACLs, or application-level encryption. Option D is managed by Amazon Route 53 or the VPC''s DNS resolver.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_67',
  'A solutions architect wants to optimize the delivery of both static and dynamic content globally, ensuring low latency for users worldwide. Which AWS service is best suited for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudFront","AWS Global Accelerator","Amazon S3","Amazon Route 53"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon CloudFront) is the correct choice. CloudFront is a content delivery network (CDN) that accelerates the delivery of both static and dynamic content by caching it at edge locations closer to users and optimizing connections to origin servers. Option B (AWS Global Accelerator) improves application availability and performance using the AWS global network but focuses on directing traffic to optimal regional endpoints, not caching content directly. Option C (Amazon S3) is object storage and can host static content but doesn''t provide the global caching and acceleration of a CDN. Option D (Amazon Route 53) is a DNS service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_68',
  'Which AWS networking component allows instances in a private subnet to connect to the internet or other AWS services, but prevents external hosts from initiating connections to those instances?',
  'mcq',
  '["NAT Gateway","Internet Gateway","VPC Endpoint","Virtual Private Gateway"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (NAT Gateway) is the correct component. A NAT Gateway allows instances in a private subnet to initiate outbound connections to the internet or other AWS services while preventing inbound connections from the internet to those instances. Option B (Internet Gateway) allows direct two-way internet access for instances in public subnets. Option C (VPC Endpoint) allows private connectivity to supported AWS services without traversing the internet. Option D (Virtual Private Gateway) is used to connect a VPC to an on-premises network via VPN or Direct Connect.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_69',
  'Which AWS service provides a scalable, fully managed, and highly available DNS web service?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Route 53","Amazon CloudFront","AWS Global Accelerator","Amazon VPC"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon Route 53) is the correct choice. Amazon Route 53 is a highly available and scalable cloud Domain Name System (DNS) web service. It translates human-readable domain names into the IP addresses that computers use to connect to each other. Option B (Amazon CloudFront) is a content delivery network. Option C (AWS Global Accelerator) improves application availability and performance. Option D (Amazon VPC) is for creating isolated network environments in AWS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_70',
  'What is the purpose of an Internet Gateway in an Amazon VPC?',
  'mcq',
  '["To allow communication between instances in a VPC and the internet","To connect a VPC to an on-premises network","To provide private connectivity to AWS services","To filter traffic between subnets"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (To allow communication between instances in a VPC and the internet) is the correct purpose of an Internet Gateway. An Internet Gateway is a horizontally scaled, redundant, and highly available VPC component that allows communication between instances in your VPC and the internet. Option B (To connect a VPC to an on-premises network) is done using a Virtual Private Gateway or AWS Direct Connect. Option C (To provide private connectivity to AWS services) is done using VPC Endpoints. Option D (To filter traffic between subnets) is done using Network ACLs or Security Groups.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_71',
  'A media streaming service is experiencing high latency for users accessing content globally, particularly for dynamic content such as personalized recommendations and user-specific dashboards. The current architecture serves all content from a single Amazon EC2 instance fleet in a single AWS Region. The company needs to improve global user experience by reducing latency and offloading traffic from the origin, without significantly re-architecting the backend application.',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement Amazon CloudFront with caching for static content and configure it to forward dynamic requests to the origin.","Deploy an Application Load Balancer (ALB) in front of the EC2 instances and configure cross-Region load balancing.","Use AWS Global Accelerator to direct user traffic to the closest healthy endpoint in the origin Region.","Migrate the EC2 instance fleet to a Region geographically closer to the majority of the user base."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective solution for improving global latency for both static and dynamic content while offloading traffic. Amazon CloudFront is a global content delivery network (CDN) that caches static content at edge locations closer to users, significantly reducing latency. For dynamic content, CloudFront optimizes the connection between the user and the origin, including persistent connections, TLS termination at the edge, and TCP optimizations, which also reduces latency. This approach addresses the anti-pattern of not using CloudFront for dynamic content (ap-3.4-002). Option B, using an ALB with cross-Region load balancing, is not a standard feature of ALB and would require complex setup, not directly addressing global latency reduction at the edge. Option C, AWS Global Accelerator, improves performance by directing traffic over the AWS global network to the closest healthy endpoint, but it primarily optimizes network pathing rather than content caching and edge processing for dynamic content like CloudFront. Option D, migrating to a closer Region, would only benefit users in that specific geographic area and would not solve global latency issues for all users.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_72',
  'A company operates a critical business application hosted in a single AWS Region. To enhance disaster recovery capabilities and provide low-latency access for users in a different geographic continent, the company decides to deploy an active-passive disaster recovery strategy, with a hot standby in a second AWS Region. They need a solution to automatically route traffic to the healthy Region in case of an outage and ensure users are always directed to the best performing endpoint.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon Route 53 with a simple routing policy to point to the primary Region''s Application Load Balancer.","Implement Amazon CloudFront with multiple origins, one for each Region, and use origin failover.","Utilize AWS Global Accelerator, configuring endpoints in both Regions and using health checks.","Set up a VPN connection between the two AWS Regions and manually switch traffic in case of failure."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most suitable solution for active-passive disaster recovery with automatic failover and performance optimization across Regions. AWS Global Accelerator provides static Anycast IP addresses that are advertised from AWS edge locations globally. It continuously monitors the health of your application endpoints in both Regions and automatically routes traffic to the closest healthy endpoint. This ensures users are always directed to the best performing endpoint and provides rapid failover in case of an outage. Option A, Amazon Route 53 with a simple routing policy, does not provide automatic health checks or failover between Regions; a failover routing policy would be needed, but Global Accelerator offers superior performance benefits by routing over the AWS backbone. Option B, Amazon CloudFront, is a CDN primarily for content delivery and caching, not for routing all application traffic for active-passive disaster recovery across Regions. Option D, a VPN connection, is for network connectivity, not for global traffic routing or automatic failover.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_73',
  'A company has a hybrid cloud architecture, with critical applications running both on-premises and in an AWS VPC. They need to establish a dedicated, private, and consistent low-latency network connection between their on-premises data center and the AWS VPC to support real-time data synchronization and application integration. The current IPsec VPN connection experiences variable latency and bandwidth, which is impacting performance.',
  'mcq',
  '["Continue using the existing IPsec VPN connection and optimize its configuration.","Implement AWS Direct Connect to establish a dedicated network connection.","Increase the internet bandwidth at the on-premises data center to improve VPN performance.","Use AWS Client VPN to allow on-premises servers to connect to the VPC."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most suitable solution. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated, private network connection from your on-premises data center to AWS. This bypasses the public internet, offering consistent low latency and high bandwidth, which is crucial for real-time data synchronization and application integration. This directly addresses the anti-pattern of using VPN for consistent low-latency connectivity when Direct Connect is available (ap-3.4-001). Option A, continuing with the existing VPN, will still suffer from variable latency and bandwidth as it traverses the public internet. Option C, increasing internet bandwidth, might improve overall internet performance but will not eliminate the inherent variability and security concerns of traversing the public internet for a private connection. Option D, AWS Client VPN, is designed for remote users to securely access AWS resources, not for site-to-site connectivity between a data center and a VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_74',
  'A company is hosting a public-facing API on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB). They want to improve the security posture by ensuring all client connections to the ALB use HTTPS, enforcing a minimum TLS version, and redirecting all HTTP requests to HTTPS. The solution should be configured directly on the load balancer to offload this from the application servers.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the ALB to listen on port 80 (HTTP) and port 443 (HTTPS), with a redirect rule from HTTP to HTTPS.","Install SSL certificates on each EC2 instance and configure the application to enforce HTTPS.","Use an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of the ALB to handle HTTPS termination and redirection.","Implement a Network Load Balancer (NLB) and configure TLS termination on the target EC2 instances."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most direct and efficient way to achieve the requirements. An Application Load Balancer (ALB) can be configured with an HTTPS listener (port 443) to terminate SSL/TLS connections, offloading this task from the EC2 instances. It can also have an HTTP listener (port 80) with a redirect action configured to automatically redirect all incoming HTTP requests to HTTPS, enforcing secure communication. Option B, installing certificates on EC2 instances, increases operational overhead and does not offload TLS termination from the application servers. Option C, using Amazon CloudFront, is a valid approach for global distribution and security, but for an application already behind an ALB in a single Region, configuring the ALB directly is simpler and sufficient for the stated requirements. Option D, a Network Load Balancer (NLB), operates at Layer 4 and does not support HTTP-to-HTTPS redirection or advanced content-based routing, and TLS termination on NLB is typically for TCP passthrough or TLS termination at the NLB itself, not for HTTP-specific redirects.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_75',
  'Which AWS service is designed to automatically distribute incoming application traffic across multiple targets, such as Amazon EC2 instances, in multiple Availability Zones, and supports path-based routing for different microservices?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Global Accelerator","Application Load Balancer (ALB)","Network Load Balancer (NLB)","Amazon CloudFront"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Application Load Balancer - ALB) is the correct service. ALBs operate at Layer 7 (the application layer) and support advanced routing features like path-based routing, host-based routing, and query string parameter-based routing, which are ideal for microservices. Option A (AWS Global Accelerator) improves the availability and performance of applications with a global user base by directing traffic to optimal endpoints over the AWS global network, but it is not a load balancer for distributing traffic to EC2 instances within a Region. Option C (Network Load Balancer - NLB) operates at Layer 4 (the transport layer) and is optimized for extreme performance and static IP addresses, but it does not support path-based routing. Option D (Amazon CloudFront) is a content delivery network (CDN) service that caches content at edge locations to reduce latency, but it is not primarily a load balancer for backend EC2 instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

