INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_57',
  'A financial application requires a database that can scale write operations across multiple primary instances in different AWS Regions, ensuring high availability and low latency for global users. Which AWS database feature supports this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Aurora Global Database with multi-master","Amazon RDS Read Replicas","Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables","Amazon ElastiCache for Redis"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon Aurora Global Database with multi-master) is the correct choice. Amazon Aurora Global Database allows a single Aurora database to span multiple AWS Regions, and with multi-master, it enables multiple primary instances across regions, supporting write scaling and high availability for global applications. Option B (Amazon RDS Read Replicas) only scales read operations, not write operations. Option C (Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables) provides multi-Region, multi-active database capabilities for NoSQL workloads, but the scenario implies a relational database context (financial application often implies relational). Option D (Amazon ElastiCache for Redis) is a caching service, not a primary database for write scaling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  95,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_58',
  'A gaming company needs a database for user profiles that experiences extremely high read and write traffic, requiring consistent single-digit millisecond latency at massive scale. Which AWS database service is best suited?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon RDS","Amazon Aurora","Amazon Redshift"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon DynamoDB) is the correct choice. Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database service designed for high-performance applications that require consistent single-digit millisecond latency at any scale, making it ideal for gaming leaderboards, user profiles, and session data. Option B (Amazon RDS) and Option C (Amazon Aurora) are relational databases that might struggle to maintain single-digit millisecond latency at massive scale for key-value access patterns. Option D (Amazon Redshift) is a data warehouse for analytics, not an OLTP database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  96,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_59',
  'Which caching strategy involves caching data close to the application layer to reduce the load on the database and improve response times for frequently accessed data?',
  'mcq',
  '["In-memory caching with Amazon ElastiCache","Database read replicas","Data warehousing with Amazon Redshift","Object storage with Amazon S3"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (In-memory caching with Amazon ElastiCache) is the correct caching strategy. Amazon ElastiCache provides in-memory data stores (Redis or Memcached) that can be deployed close to application servers to cache frequently accessed data, significantly reducing database load and improving application response times. Option B (Database read replicas) scale read capacity but don''t cache query results to reduce load on the primary. Option C (Data warehousing with Amazon Redshift) is for analytics, not operational caching. Option D (Object storage with Amazon S3) is for general data storage, not an in-memory caching solution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  97,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_60',
  'A company needs to store customer session data that requires very fast read/write access and can tolerate some data loss in case of node failure. Which Amazon ElastiCache engine is suitable?',
  'mcq',
  '["Memcached","Redis","DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","Amazon Aurora"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Memcached) is suitable for this use case. Memcached is a simpler caching system that is ideal for caching session data where data loss is acceptable, and horizontal scaling is a priority. It is often used for simple key-value lookups. Option B (Redis) offers more advanced features like persistence, replication, and data structures, making it suitable when data durability and more complex use cases are required. Option C (DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)) is a caching service specifically for Amazon DynamoDB, not a general-purpose cache. Option D (Amazon Aurora) is a relational database, not a caching service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  98,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_61',
  'What is the primary purpose of Amazon RDS?',
  'mcq',
  '["To provide managed relational database services","To provide NoSQL database services","To provide in-memory caching services","To provide data warehousing services"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (To provide managed relational database services) is the primary purpose of Amazon RDS (Relational Database Service). RDS makes it easy to set up, operate, and scale a relational database in the cloud, supporting various database engines. Option B (To provide NoSQL database services) is the purpose of Amazon DynamoDB. Option C (To provide in-memory caching services) is the purpose of Amazon ElastiCache. Option D (To provide data warehousing services) is the purpose of Amazon Redshift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  99,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_62',
  'Which AWS service is a managed, in-memory caching service that supports both Redis and Memcached engines?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon ElastiCache","Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","Amazon RDS Proxy","Amazon Aurora"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon ElastiCache) is the correct choice. Amazon ElastiCache is a fully managed, in-memory caching service that supports two open-source in-memory engines: Redis and Memcached. It helps improve application performance by retrieving data from fast in-memory caches instead of relying entirely on slower disk-based databases. Option B (Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)) is a caching service specifically for DynamoDB. Option C (Amazon RDS Proxy) is a connection pooling service for RDS. Option D (Amazon Aurora) is a relational database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  100,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_63',
  'What is the main benefit of using Amazon RDS Read Replicas?',
  'mcq',
  '["To scale read-heavy database workloads","To scale write-heavy database workloads","To cache frequently accessed data in memory","To reduce database connection overhead from serverless functions"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (To scale read-heavy database workloads) is the main benefit of Amazon RDS Read Replicas. Read replicas provide an independent copy of your primary database that can handle read traffic, offloading the primary instance and improving performance for read-intensive applications. Option B (To scale write-heavy database workloads) is incorrect, as read replicas do not handle write scaling. Option C (To cache frequently accessed data in memory) is a function of Amazon ElastiCache. Option D (To reduce database connection overhead from serverless functions) is a function of Amazon RDS Proxy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  101,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

