INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_65',
  'A company needs to store customer data that requires a flexible schema and can handle high volumes of unstructured data with low latency. Which AWS database service should they consider?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon RDS","Amazon Aurora","Amazon Redshift"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon DynamoDB) is the correct choice. Amazon DynamoDB is a NoSQL key-value and document database that offers a flexible schema, can handle high volumes of unstructured or semi-structured data, and provides single-digit millisecond latency at any scale. Option B (Amazon RDS) and Option C (Amazon Aurora) are relational databases with rigid schemas. Option D (Amazon Redshift) is a data warehouse for structured analytical data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  103,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_66',
  'A popular social media application uses Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL as its primary database. The application experiences periods of extremely high read traffic during viral events, leading to database performance bottlenecks. The development team wants to offload read traffic from the primary database to improve response times and reduce the load on the primary instance, without modifying the application code extensively. The solution must be highly available and scalable.',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the instance size of the primary Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database.","Implement Amazon RDS read replicas for PostgreSQL and configure the application to direct read queries to them.","Migrate the database to Amazon DynamoDB to leverage its high read/write throughput.","Deploy Amazon ElastiCache for Redis to cache frequently accessed data."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most direct and effective solution for offloading read traffic from the primary Amazon RDS instance. Amazon RDS read replicas allow you to create one or more read-only copies of your primary database instance. The application can then be configured to direct read queries to these replicas, distributing the read load and improving performance. This is a standard pattern for scaling read-heavy workloads in relational databases. Option A, increasing the instance size, might provide some temporary relief but is not a scalable solution for read-heavy workloads and will increase costs without addressing the architectural pattern for read scaling. Option C, migrating to Amazon DynamoDB, would require significant application re-architecture due to the fundamental differences between relational and NoSQL databases, and is not suitable for complex relational queries (ap-3.3-002). Option D, deploying Amazon ElastiCache for Redis, is an excellent caching strategy (ap-3.3-003) for frequently accessed data, but it requires application code changes to implement caching logic and might not be sufficient for all read-heavy scenarios where the data is not consistently cached or requires fresh reads.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  104,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_67',
  'A rapidly growing mobile gaming company uses Amazon RDS for MySQL as its backend database. The application''s architecture involves numerous AWS Lambda functions that frequently connect to the database to retrieve and update player data. The company observes occasional ''Too many connections'' errors during peak traffic, indicating that the Lambda functions are exhausting the database''s connection limits. The team wants to resolve this issue with minimal code changes and operational overhead.',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the `max_connections` parameter in the Amazon RDS instance parameter group.","Implement Amazon RDS Proxy to manage and pool database connections for Lambda functions.","Migrate the player data to Amazon DynamoDB to eliminate connection limits.","Reduce the concurrency limit of the AWS Lambda functions connecting to the database."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most effective and recommended solution. Amazon RDS Proxy is a fully managed, highly available database proxy that makes applications more resilient to database failures and more secure. It pools and reuses database connections, preventing connection exhaustion from frequently invoked AWS Lambda functions (ap-3.3-004) and reducing the load on the database. It requires minimal application code changes. Option A, increasing `max_connections`, is a temporary fix that can lead to performance degradation if the database is overloaded by too many active connections, and it doesn''t solve the fundamental problem of short-lived Lambda connections. Option C, migrating to Amazon DynamoDB, would require significant re-architecture of the application and is not suitable for complex relational queries (ap-3.3-002). Option D, reducing Lambda concurrency, would limit the application''s scalability and might not be feasible during peak traffic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  105,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_68',
  'A global IoT platform collects sensor data from millions of devices, generating a continuous stream of small, high-velocity data points. This data needs to be stored in a highly scalable, low-latency database that can handle millions of writes per second and support simple key-value lookups for real-time dashboards. The data model is non-relational, and the company wants a fully managed solution to minimize operational overhead.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL with multiple read replicas for high write throughput.","Utilize Amazon DynamoDB, configuring on-demand capacity mode for flexible scaling.","Set up Amazon Redshift for real-time data ingestion and querying.","Implement a self-managed Apache Cassandra cluster on Amazon EC2 instances."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most suitable solution. Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL key-value and document database designed for high-performance applications at any scale. It can handle millions of requests per second with single-digit millisecond latency, making it ideal for high-velocity IoT sensor data and real-time dashboards. On-demand capacity mode automatically scales read/write capacity, minimizing operational overhead. Option A, Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, is a relational database and is not designed for millions of writes per second for simple key-value lookups; read replicas scale reads, not writes (ap-3.3-001). Option C, Amazon Redshift, is a data warehouse optimized for analytical queries on large datasets, not for high-volume, low-latency transactional writes. Option D, a self-managed Apache Cassandra cluster on EC2, would incur significant operational overhead for deployment, scaling, and maintenance, which the company wants to minimize.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  106,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_69',
  'A data analytics platform uses Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL-Compatible Edition as its primary database. Analysts frequently run complex, ad-hoc analytical queries that involve multiple joins across large tables, which often strain the primary database instance and impact the performance of transactional workloads. The company needs to offload these analytical queries to a separate, scalable data store optimized for complex analytics, without affecting the operational database.',
  'mcq',
  '["Create Amazon Aurora Replicas and direct analytical queries to them.","Migrate the analytical data to Amazon DynamoDB for improved query performance.","Use Amazon Redshift to store and query the analytical data, replicating from Aurora.","Increase the instance size of the primary Amazon Aurora database to handle the load."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most appropriate solution. Amazon Redshift is a fully managed, petabyte-scale cloud data warehouse service optimized for complex analytical queries on large datasets. By replicating the necessary data from Amazon Aurora to Amazon Redshift, the analytical queries can be offloaded entirely, preventing them from impacting the performance of the operational Aurora database. Option A, Aurora Replicas, are designed for scaling read-heavy transactional workloads, not for complex, ad-hoc analytical queries that are better suited for a data warehouse. Option B, Amazon DynamoDB, is a NoSQL key-value/document store and is not suitable for complex relational joins and analytical queries (ap-3.3-002). Option D, increasing the primary instance size, is a vertical scaling approach that would temporarily alleviate the issue but is not a scalable or cost-effective solution for dedicated analytical workloads and would still combine transactional and analytical processing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  107,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_70',
  'A global real-time bidding (RTB) platform requires an extremely low-latency, high-throughput data store for storing user profiles and bidding preferences. This data is frequently read and updated, with millions of read/write operations per second. The data model is simple key-value, and the platform needs sub-millisecond response times. The solution must be fully managed to minimize operational overhead.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy Amazon RDS for MySQL with a large instance type and read replicas.","Utilize Amazon DynamoDB with provisioned capacity mode for consistent performance.","Implement Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached to store user profiles.","Set up a self-managed Apache Cassandra cluster on Amazon EC2 instances."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most suitable solution. Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL key-value and document database designed for high-performance applications at any scale, offering single-digit millisecond latency for millions of requests per second. Its key-value model perfectly fits user profiles and bidding preferences. Provisioned capacity mode ensures consistent high throughput. Option A, Amazon RDS for MySQL, is a relational database and is not designed for the extreme scale of millions of read/write operations per second with sub-millisecond latency for simple key-value lookups (ap-3.3-002). Option C, Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached, is an in-memory caching service that could be used in conjunction with DynamoDB, but it is not a primary data store for durable, high-volume writes. Option D, a self-managed Apache Cassandra cluster on EC2, would incur significant operational overhead for deployment, scaling, and maintenance, which the company wants to minimize.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  108,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_71',
  'Which AWS database service is best suited for highly scalable, non-relational key-value and document workloads requiring single-digit millisecond performance at any scale?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL","Amazon Aurora","Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached","Amazon DynamoDB"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (Amazon DynamoDB) is the correct choice. It is a fully managed NoSQL database service that supports key-value and document data models, offering consistent single-digit millisecond latency at any scale. Option A (Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL) and Option B (Amazon Aurora) are relational database services, which are not designed for non-relational key-value or document workloads, nor do they typically offer the same level of consistent low-latency scalability as DynamoDB for these specific access patterns. Option C (Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached) is an in-memory caching service, primarily used to improve application performance by caching frequently accessed data, not as a primary persistent database for key-value workloads.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  109,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

