INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_48',
  'A company needs to run a batch processing workload that can tolerate interruptions and wants to minimize compute costs. Which EC2 purchasing option should they choose?',
  'mcq',
  '["On-Demand Instances","Reserved Instances","Spot Instances","Dedicated Hosts"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Spot Instances) are the most cost-effective option for fault-tolerant batch processing workloads that can handle interruptions, as they allow users to bid for unused EC2 capacity at significantly reduced prices. Option A (On-Demand Instances) are more expensive and do not offer cost savings for interruptible workloads. Option B (Reserved Instances) are for steady-state, predictable workloads and require a commitment, which isn''t ideal for interruptible tasks. Option D (Dedicated Hosts) provide physical servers for compliance or licensing, which is much more expensive and not relevant for cost optimization of interruptible batch jobs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_49',
  'What is the primary advantage of using AWS Lambda for infrequent, event-driven tasks?',
  'mcq',
  '["Guaranteed instance uptime","Lower operational overhead due to serverless execution","Full control over the underlying operating system","Ability to run long-running, stateful applications"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Lower operational overhead due to serverless execution) is the primary advantage of AWS Lambda for infrequent, event-driven tasks. Lambda automatically manages the compute resources, patching, and scaling, significantly reducing operational burden. Option A (Guaranteed instance uptime) is not applicable as Lambda is serverless; there are no instances to guarantee uptime for in the traditional sense. Option C (Full control over the underlying operating system) is incorrect; Lambda abstracts away the OS. Option D (Ability to run long-running, stateful applications) is generally not ideal for Lambda, which is designed for short-lived, stateless functions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_50',
  'Which AWS service allows you to run code in response to events without provisioning or managing servers?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2","AWS Lambda","Amazon RDS","AWS Fargate"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Lambda) is a serverless compute service that allows you to run code without provisioning or managing servers. It executes your code only when needed and scales automatically. Option A (Amazon EC2) requires you to provision and manage virtual servers. Option C (Amazon RDS) is a managed relational database service. Option D (AWS Fargate) is a serverless compute engine for containers, not directly for arbitrary code functions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_51',
  'What is the primary benefit of using EC2 Spot Instances for fault-tolerant workloads?',
  'mcq',
  '["Significant cost savings compared to On-Demand instances.","Guaranteed capacity availability without interruptions.","Consistent performance for critical production applications.","Automatic scaling based on application load with no manual configuration."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) states that EC2 Spot Instances offer significant cost savings compared to On-Demand instances. This is their primary benefit, making them ideal for fault-tolerant workloads that can handle interruptions. Option B (incorrect) is false; Spot Instances can be interrupted by AWS with a two-minute notification. Option C (incorrect) is not the primary benefit; while performance can be consistent when running, their interruptible nature makes them unsuitable for critical production applications that cannot tolerate interruptions. Option D (incorrect) describes Auto Scaling, which can be used with Spot Instances but is not an inherent feature of Spot Instances themselves.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_52',
  'What is the main advantage of using AWS Lambda for event-driven applications from a cost optimization perspective?',
  'mcq',
  '["It allows for manual scaling of compute resources to match demand.","It provides dedicated servers that can be provisioned for peak performance.","It enables paying only for the compute time consumed, eliminating idle costs.","It offers a fixed monthly cost for unlimited function invocations."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) highlights the ''pay-per-use'' model of AWS Lambda, where you are billed only for the compute time consumed when your functions are actively running, rounded up to the nearest millisecond. This eliminates costs associated with idle servers. Option A (incorrect) is false; Lambda automatically scales compute resources without manual intervention. Option B (incorrect) is incorrect; Lambda is a serverless service, meaning users do not provision or manage dedicated servers. Option D (incorrect) is false; Lambda costs are based on the number of requests and the duration of execution, not a fixed monthly cost for unlimited invocations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_53',
  'What is a key cost-optimization benefit of using AWS Graviton processors for compatible workloads?',
  'mcq',
  '["They offer higher clock speeds for single-threaded applications compared to x86 processors.","They provide up to 40% better price/performance over comparable x86 instances.","They are exclusively designed for Windows-based workloads, reducing licensing costs.","They eliminate the need for Auto Scaling by providing inherent elastic capacity."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) states that AWS Graviton instances, powered by ARM-based processors, can provide up to 40% better price/performance over comparable x86 instances for many Linux-based workloads. This makes them a significant cost optimization for compatible applications. Option A (incorrect) is generally false; Graviton processors are known for efficiency and multi-core performance, not necessarily higher single-threaded clock speeds. Option C (incorrect) is false; Graviton instances primarily support Linux-based workloads, not exclusively Windows. Option D (incorrect) is false; Graviton instances still benefit from Auto Scaling for elasticity, they do not inherently eliminate the need for it.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_54',
  'Which AWS service allows you to run containers without managing the underlying EC2 instances?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Fargate.","Amazon EC2.","Amazon RDS.","AWS CloudFormation."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS Fargate, a serverless compute engine for containers that works with Amazon ECS and Amazon EKS. It allows you to run containers without having to provision, manage, or scale EC2 instances. Option B (incorrect) Amazon EC2 requires you to provision and manage virtual servers. Option C (incorrect) Amazon RDS is a managed relational database service. Option D (incorrect) AWS CloudFormation is an infrastructure as code service for provisioning AWS resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_55',
  'What is the purpose of AWS Savings Plans in optimizing compute costs?',
  'mcq',
  '["To provide fixed discounts for specific EC2 instance types and Availability Zones.","To offer flexible discounts for consistent compute usage across various services and configurations.","To enable bidding on unused EC2 capacity for significant cost reductions.","To automatically shut down idle EC2 instances during off-peak hours."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes AWS Savings Plans. They offer a flexible pricing model that provides significant savings over On-Demand prices in exchange for a commitment to a consistent amount of compute usage (measured in USD/hour) for a 1- or 3-year term. This commitment applies across different instance families, sizes, operating systems, and even AWS Regions, offering more flexibility than Reserved Instances. Option A (incorrect) describes EC2 Reserved Instances, which are less flexible. Option C (incorrect) describes EC2 Spot Instances. Option D (incorrect) describes a strategy for managing idle environments, often implemented with services like AWS Lambda or AWS Instance Scheduler, not Savings Plans themselves.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_56',
  'A development team needs to run several non-production EC2 instances for 8 hours a day during weekdays. The team wants to minimize costs associated with idle compute. Which approach is MOST cost-effective?',
  'mcq',
  '["Purchase 1-year EC2 Reserved Instances for all development instances.","Run all development instances 24/7 using On-Demand pricing.","Implement an automated schedule to start instances at the beginning of the workday and stop them at the end.","Utilize EC2 Spot Instances for all development workloads, regardless of interruption tolerance."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most cost-effective approach. By implementing an automated schedule to start and stop instances, the company only pays for the compute resources when they are actively used, eliminating costs during idle periods (e.g., nights and weekends). Option A (incorrect) is suboptimal because Reserved Instances are for steady-state usage, and committing to 24/7 usage for instances only needed 8 hours a day would lead to paying for unused capacity (anti-pattern: running idle development environments 24/7). Option B (incorrect) running instances 24/7 on On-Demand pricing is highly inefficient as it incurs costs even when instances are idle. Option D (incorrect) while Spot Instances offer savings, they are interruptible and may not be suitable for all development workloads, especially if state needs to be preserved or work cannot be easily restarted.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_57',
  'A startup is launching a new web application with unpredictable traffic patterns, expecting significant spikes. They want to ensure high availability and cost-efficiency. Which compute strategy is MOST suitable?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy a single large Amazon EC2 instance to handle peak load.","Use AWS Lambda for the backend logic and Amazon API Gateway for endpoints.","Provision a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances with a fixed capacity.","Utilize EC2 Reserved Instances for the entire expected workload."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) using AWS Lambda for backend logic and Amazon API Gateway for endpoints provides a serverless architecture that automatically scales to handle unpredictable traffic spikes without requiring manual provisioning or management of servers. This approach is highly cost-effective as you only pay for the compute time consumed. Option A (incorrect) deploying a single large EC2 instance creates a single point of failure and is not cost-effective for unpredictable spikes, as it would be over-provisioned during low traffic (anti-pattern: choosing a single large instance instead of multiple smaller instances with Auto Scaling, though Lambda is even better here). Option C (incorrect) provisioning a fixed fleet of EC2 instances would either be over-provisioned for average load or under-provisioned for spikes, leading to inefficiency or poor performance. Option D (incorrect) EC2 Reserved Instances are for predictable, steady-state workloads, not for unpredictable traffic patterns.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_58',
  'A company is migrating an existing application to AWS that is compatible with ARM processors. They want to achieve the best price/performance ratio for their compute resources. Which EC2 instance type should they prioritize?',
  'mcq',
  '["x86-based compute optimized instances (e.g., C5, C6i).","AWS Graviton-based instances (e.g., M6g, C7g).","Memory optimized instances (e.g., R5, R6i).","Storage optimized instances (e.g., I3, I4i)."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) AWS Graviton-based instances (e.g., M6g, C7g) are powered by ARM processors and are specifically designed to provide up to 40% better price/performance over comparable x86 instances for a wide range of workloads. Since the application is compatible with ARM, this is the most cost-effective choice for price/performance (anti-pattern: not considering Graviton (ARM) instances for compatible workloads). Option A (incorrect) x86-based compute optimized instances are powerful but generally do not offer the same price/performance benefits as Graviton for compatible ARM workloads. Option C (incorrect) Memory optimized instances are for workloads requiring large amounts of RAM. Option D (incorrect) Storage optimized instances are for workloads that require high, sequential read and write access to very large datasets on local storage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_59',
  'Which AWS cost management tool allows you to visualize, understand, and manage your AWS costs and usage over time?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Budgets.","AWS Cost Explorer.","AWS Trusted Advisor.","AWS Organizations."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) AWS Cost Explorer is a tool that enables you to visualize, understand, and manage your AWS costs and usage over time. You can analyze your data at a high level (e.g., total costs and usage across all accounts) or dive deeper into your costs to identify trends and cost drivers. Option A (incorrect) AWS Budgets allows you to set custom budgets and receive alerts when your costs or usage exceed (or are forecasted to exceed) your budgeted amount. Option C (incorrect) AWS Trusted Advisor provides recommendations to help you follow AWS best practices, including cost optimization, but it''s not primarily a cost visualization tool. Option D (incorrect) AWS Organizations helps you centrally manage multiple AWS accounts, but it''s not a cost analysis tool itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_60',
  'A company wants to receive alerts when their monthly AWS spend exceeds a predefined threshold. Which AWS service should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Cost Explorer to analyze historical trends.","AWS Budgets to set custom cost and usage alerts.","AWS Cost and Usage Report for detailed billing data.","Amazon CloudWatch for resource-level monitoring."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) AWS Budgets allows you to set custom budgets to track your costs and usage and receive alerts when your actual or forecasted costs exceed your defined thresholds. This directly addresses the requirement of receiving alerts for exceeding a monthly spend threshold. Option A (incorrect) AWS Cost Explorer is for visualizing and analyzing costs, not for setting alerts. Option C (incorrect) AWS Cost and Usage Report provides comprehensive billing data but does not offer real-time alerting. Option D (incorrect) Amazon CloudWatch monitors resources and applications, but AWS Budgets is specifically designed for overall cost and usage thresholds.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_61',
  'Which AWS cost management service feature helps allocate costs to different departments or projects by labeling resources?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Budgets.","Cost allocation tags.","AWS Organizations.","AWS Cost Explorer."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) Cost allocation tags are labels that you can apply to your AWS resources to categorize and track your costs. By tagging resources, you can generate detailed cost reports that help allocate costs to specific departments, projects, or environments. Option A (incorrect) AWS Budgets is for setting spending thresholds and alerts. Option C (incorrect) AWS Organizations helps manage multiple AWS accounts. Option D (incorrect) AWS Cost Explorer is a visualization tool that can use tags for filtering, but tags themselves are the feature for allocation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_62',
  'An e-commerce platform experiences predictable traffic patterns, with a consistent baseline load and regular, significant spikes during promotional events. The company has a 1-year commitment to AWS and aims to optimize compute costs for its Amazon EC2 fleet. The workload is distributed across multiple instance types and families.',
  'mcq',
  '["Purchase Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances for the baseline capacity of specific instance types and sizes.","Utilize AWS Savings Plans to cover the baseline compute usage across various instance types and families, and use On-Demand for spikes.","Use Amazon EC2 Spot Instances for the entire fleet, leveraging their deep discounts.","Implement a single large Amazon EC2 instance to handle the maximum peak load, avoiding the complexity of multiple instances."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the most cost-effective and flexible solution for a predictable workload with a 1-year commitment, especially when dealing with multiple instance types and families. AWS Savings Plans provide significant discounts (up to 72%) in exchange for a commitment to a consistent amount of compute usage (measured in USD per hour) over a 1-year or 3-year term. They offer flexibility across instance families, sizes, Availability Zones, and even AWS Regions. This allows the company to optimize costs for its baseline usage across diverse instance types while still having the flexibility to scale with On-Demand Instances for predictable spikes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_63',
  'A data processing application performs intensive computations and requires high CPU performance. The application is primarily Linux-based and has been verified to be compatible with ARM architecture. The company wants to minimize compute costs while maximizing performance for this specific workload.',
  'mcq',
  '["Select the latest generation x86-based compute-optimized Amazon EC2 instances (e.g., C6i).","Select AWS Graviton-based Amazon EC2 instances (e.g., C7g), as they offer superior price/performance for compatible workloads.","Select memory-optimized Amazon EC2 instances (e.g., R6i), as they provide large amounts of RAM for data processing.","Select Amazon EC2 Spot Instances of any type, as they offer the lowest cost regardless of architecture."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the most cost-effective and performance-optimized solution for this scenario. AWS Graviton-based Amazon EC2 instances (ARM architecture) provide up to 40% better price/performance over comparable x86 instances for a wide range of workloads, including compute-intensive tasks. Since the application is Linux-based and compatible with ARM, leveraging Graviton instances directly addresses the anti-pattern of not considering Graviton (ARM) instances for compatible workloads, ensuring maximum cost savings and performance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_64',
  'A development team maintains several non-production environments for testing and staging. These environments are only active during working hours (Monday to Friday, 9 AM to 6 PM) and remain idle otherwise. The company wants to implement a cost-optimization strategy to reduce the compute costs associated with these environments.',
  'mcq',
  '["Purchase Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances for all non-production instances to achieve significant discounts.","Implement a manual process for developers to stop and start their EC2 instances at the beginning and end of each workday.","Use AWS Instance Scheduler or a custom AWS Lambda solution to automatically stop EC2 instances outside working hours and start them again at the beginning of the workday.","Migrate all non-production workloads to AWS Fargate to leverage serverless computing, eliminating idle costs."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is the most effective and automated cost-optimization strategy. AWS Instance Scheduler or a custom AWS Lambda solution can automatically stop EC2 instances during non-working hours and start them again when needed. This eliminates compute costs for idle periods, significantly reducing the overall expenditure for non-production environments. Manual processes (Option B) are prone to human error and inconsistency, while Reserved Instances (Option A) are not suitable for intermittent workloads and Fargate (Option D) might involve significant re-architecture for existing EC2-based applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_65',
  'A company is migrating its on-premises data processing application to AWS. The application is highly CPU-intensive and runs on Linux. Initial testing shows that the application is compatible with ARM processors. The company''s priority is to achieve the best price/performance ratio for its compute resources.',
  'mcq',
  '["Choose the latest generation x86-based compute-optimized Amazon EC2 instances (e.g., C6i) to ensure broad software compatibility.","Choose AWS Graviton-based Amazon EC2 instances (e.g., C7g) due to their superior price/performance for compatible Linux workloads.","Choose memory-optimized Amazon EC2 instances (e.g., R6i) to handle large datasets efficiently.","Choose Amazon EC2 Spot Instances of x86 architecture to minimize costs, regardless of performance."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the most effective choice for achieving the best price/performance ratio given the application''s compatibility with ARM. AWS Graviton-based Amazon EC2 instances offer up to 40% better price/performance compared to comparable x86 instances for a wide range of Linux-based workloads. Since the application is CPU-intensive and compatible with ARM, leveraging Graviton instances directly addresses the anti-pattern of not considering Graviton (ARM) instances for compatible workloads, leading to significant cost savings without compromising performance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_66',
  'A global IoT platform collects millions of data points per second. This data needs to be processed in real-time by a serverless compute service that can scale massively and execute code for individual events without managing servers. The company wants to minimize operational overhead and only pay for the actual compute duration.',
  'mcq',
  '["Provision a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group to handle the fluctuating load.","Deploy the processing logic as AWS Lambda functions, triggered by incoming IoT data.","Use AWS Fargate to run containerized processing applications, managing scaling with Amazon ECS.","Use Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances for a 3-year term to reduce compute costs for the baseline load."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the most suitable and cost-effective serverless solution for this scenario. AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that allows you to run code without provisioning or managing servers. It automatically scales to handle millions of requests per second and you only pay for the compute time consumed. This perfectly aligns with the requirement for real-time processing of individual events, massive scalability, minimal operational overhead, and a pay-per-execution cost model.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

