INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_112',
  'A public subnet hosting web servers requires an additional layer of network security to explicitly deny certain inbound and outbound traffic. Order the steps to configure a Network ACL (NACL).',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new Network ACL.","Associate the NACL with the public subnet.","Add inbound and outbound deny rules for specific problematic IP addresses or ports.","Ensure default allow rules are in place for necessary traffic after deny rules."]'::json,
  '{0,2,3,1}'::int[],
  'The first step (Option A) is to create a new Network ACL. Next, add specific inbound and outbound deny rules (Option C) for any problematic IP addresses or ports that should be blocked. Since NACLs are stateless, ensure that necessary default allow rules (Option D) are explicitly configured after any deny rules to permit legitimate traffic. Finally, associate the configured NACL (Option B) with the public subnet.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_113',
  'A new mobile application needs a scalable and secure way for users to sign up and sign in. Order the steps to set up an Amazon Cognito User Pool for user authentication.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new Amazon Cognito User Pool.","Integrate the User Pool with the mobile application.","Configure attributes, password policies, and MFA for the User Pool.","Create an application client within the User Pool."]'::json,
  '{0,2,3,1}'::int[],
  'The initial step (Option A) is to create a new Amazon Cognito User Pool. Next, configure essential settings (Option C) such as user attributes, password policies, and Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) requirements. Then, create an application client (Option D) within the User Pool, which the mobile application will use to interact with Cognito. Finally, integrate the User Pool (Option B) into the mobile application''s authentication flow.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_114',
  'A public-facing web application hosted on an Application Load Balancer (ALB) needs protection against common web exploits. Order the steps to enable AWS WAF.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new AWS WAF Web ACL.","Associate the Web ACL with the Application Load Balancer.","Add managed rule groups or custom rules to the Web ACL to define protection logic.","Test the WAF rules by attempting to trigger them with malicious requests."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}'::int[],
  'The first step (Option A) is to create a new AWS WAF Web ACL. Next, add managed rule groups or custom rules (Option C) to the Web ACL to define the specific protection logic against common web exploits. Then, associate the Web ACL (Option B) with the Application Load Balancer that fronts the web application. Finally, test the WAF rules (Option D) to ensure they are functioning as expected and blocking malicious requests.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_115',
  'A company wants to ensure its public-facing resources are protected against common network and transport layer DDoS attacks. Order the steps to leverage AWS Shield Standard.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deploy public-facing resources such as Elastic Load Balancers or Amazon CloudFront distributions.","AWS Shield Standard automatically protects these resources at no additional cost.","Monitor for potential DDoS events using Amazon CloudWatch metrics and AWS WAF logs.","Review the AWS Shield console for any detected events."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}'::int[],
  'The initial step (Option A) is to deploy public-facing resources like Elastic Load Balancers or Amazon CloudFront distributions, as these are the services that AWS Shield Standard protects. AWS Shield Standard (Option B) is automatically enabled for all AWS accounts and provides baseline DDoS protection for these resources at no additional cost. Next, review the AWS Shield console (Option D) for any detected DDoS events. Finally, monitor for potential DDoS events (Option C) using Amazon CloudWatch metrics and AWS WAF logs for deeper insights.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_116',
  'A company is deploying a public-facing web application on Amazon EC2 instances within a public subnet. To enhance security, they want to implement defense-in-depth using both security groups and Network ACLs. Order the following steps for this configuration.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a security group allowing inbound HTTP/HTTPS traffic from the internet to the EC2 instances.","Create a Network ACL (NACL) for the public subnet to allow inbound HTTP/HTTPS and outbound ephemeral ports.","Launch the Amazon EC2 instances into the public subnet.","Create the VPC and a public subnet."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}'::int[],
  'Setting up network security layers starts with the foundational network infrastructure. Option D (Create the VPC and a public subnet) is the prerequisite. Next, Network ACLs operate at the subnet level, so Option B (Create a Network ACL (NACL) for the public subnet to allow inbound HTTP/HTTPS and outbound ephemeral ports) should be configured before instance-level security. Then, Security Groups operate at the instance level, so Option A (Create a security group allowing inbound HTTP/HTTPS traffic from the internet to the EC2 instances) is the next step. Finally, the EC2 instances can be launched into this secured environment, making Option C (Launch the Amazon EC2 instances into the public subnet) the last step.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_117',
  'A solutions architect needs to protect a public-facing web application hosted on an Application Load Balancer (ALB) from common web exploits. Order the steps to implement AWS WAF for this protection.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a Web ACL (Access Control List) in AWS WAF.","Add rules to the Web ACL to filter malicious traffic.","Associate the Web ACL with the Application Load Balancer.","Monitor the Web ACL for blocked requests and adjust rules as needed."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'Implementing AWS WAF for protection follows a clear sequence. First, you need a container for your rules, so Option A (Create a Web ACL (Access Control List) in AWS WAF) is the initial step. Once the Web ACL exists, you define the logic for filtering traffic, making Option B (Add rules to the Web ACL to filter malicious traffic) the next step. For the rules to take effect, the Web ACL must be linked to a protected resource, so Option C (Associate the Web ACL with the Application Load Balancer) comes next. Finally, continuous monitoring and refinement are essential for effective security, making Option D (Monitor the Web ACL for blocked requests and adjust rules as needed) the ongoing operational step.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_118',
  'A new application needs to securely access an Amazon RDS database. The solutions architect wants to store and manage the database credentials using AWS Secrets Manager to ensure automatic rotation and secure retrieval. Order the steps to implement this solution.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the application code to retrieve the database credentials from AWS Secrets Manager.","Store the database credentials as a new secret in AWS Secrets Manager.","Grant the application''s IAM role permissions to retrieve the specific secret from Secrets Manager.","Enable automatic rotation for the secret in AWS Secrets Manager."]'::json,
  '{1,3,2,0}'::int[],
  'To securely manage database credentials with AWS Secrets Manager, the first step is to store the secret. Option B (Store the database credentials as a new secret in AWS Secrets Manager) initializes the process. For enhanced security, automatic rotation should be enabled next, making Option D (Enable automatic rotation for the secret in AWS Secrets Manager) the logical follow-up. Before the application can access the secret, it needs explicit permission, so Option C (Grant the application''s IAM role permissions to retrieve the specific secret from Secrets Manager) is critical. Finally, the application code must be updated to use Secrets Manager for retrieval, making Option A (Configure the application code to retrieve the database credentials from AWS Secrets Manager) the last step.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_119',
  'A solutions architect needs to provide secure shell (SSH) access to Amazon EC2 instances while adhering to the principle of least privilege and minimizing exposure. Order the following approaches from most secure/recommended to least secure/recommended for daily operational access.',
  'ordering',
  '["Use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager for shell access.","Use a bastion host with a security group restricted to specific administrator IPs.","Open port 22 (SSH) to 0.0.0.0/0 in the instance''s security group.","Directly SSH into the instance using a security group allowing access from a broad IP range."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}'::int[],
  'When ordering SSH access methods by security and best practices, AWS Systems Manager Session Manager is the most recommended. Option A (Use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager for shell access) provides secure, auditable access without opening inbound ports. Next, Option B (Use a bastion host with a security group restricted to specific administrator IPs) is a secure traditional method, centralizing access and limiting exposure. Option D (Directly SSH into the instance using a security group allowing access from a broad IP range) is less secure as it directly exposes the instance and a broader IP range increases the attack surface. Option C (Open port 22 (SSH) to 0.0.0.0/0 in the instance''s security group) is an anti-pattern and the least secure option, exposing the instance to the entire internet.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_120',
  'A solutions architect needs to design a secure VPC with public and private subnets for a web application. Order the steps to correctly set up this network architecture.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new VPC with a specified CIDR block.","Create public and private subnets within the VPC.","Create an Internet Gateway and attach it to the VPC.","Configure route tables for public subnets to route traffic through the Internet Gateway and for private subnets to route outbound traffic through a NAT Gateway (if needed)."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'The foundational step for any VPC architecture is to create the VPC itself with its CIDR block (Option A). Once the VPC exists, you can define its logical segments by creating public and private subnets (Option B). For public internet access, an Internet Gateway is required and must be attached to the VPC (Option C). Finally, route tables are configured: public subnets route internet-bound traffic to the Internet Gateway, and private subnets route outbound traffic through a NAT Gateway (which would be created and configured before this step, but the routing is the final configuration here) (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_121',
  'A developer needs to configure a Security Group for an Amazon EC2 instance to allow inbound HTTP and HTTPS traffic from the internet. Order the steps to achieve this.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to the EC2 dashboard in the AWS Management Console.","Select ''Security Groups'' under ''Network & Security'' and click ''Create security group''.","Add inbound rules for HTTP (port 80) and HTTPS (port 443), setting the source to ''Anywhere-IPv4'' (0.0.0.0/0).","Associate the new security group with the target EC2 instance."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'To configure a Security Group, first, navigate to the EC2 dashboard (Option A). Then, select ''Security Groups'' and initiate the creation of a new security group (Option B). The crucial step is to add inbound rules for the required protocols and ports (HTTP on 80, HTTPS on 443) and specify the source as 0.0.0.0/0 for internet access (Option C). Finally, this newly configured security group must be associated with the EC2 instance to apply the rules (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_122',
  'A company wants to protect its public-facing Application Load Balancer (ALB) from common web exploits and bots. Order the steps to configure AWS WAF for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to the AWS WAF & Shield console.","Create a new Web ACL and specify the region and the associated AWS resource (the ALB).","Add managed rule groups (e.g., AWS Managed Rules for Common Vulnerabilities) and/or custom rules to the Web ACL.","Set the default action for requests that don''t match any rules (e.g., Allow or Block)."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'To configure AWS WAF, begin by navigating to the AWS WAF & Shield console (Option A). The next step is to create a new Web ACL, specifying the region and associating it with the Application Load Balancer that needs protection (Option B). After the Web ACL is created, you add rules to it, which can include AWS Managed Rule Groups for common threats or custom rules tailored to specific needs (Option C). Finally, you must define the default action for requests that do not match any of the configured rules, typically ''Allow'' or ''Block'' (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_123',
  'A web application needs to store database credentials securely and rotate them automatically to meet compliance requirements. Order the steps to implement this using AWS Secrets Manager.',
  'ordering',
  '["Store the database credentials as a new secret in AWS Secrets Manager.","Configure automatic rotation for the secret, specifying a rotation frequency and a Lambda function to handle the rotation logic.","Modify the application code to retrieve the database credentials from AWS Secrets Manager at runtime instead of using hardcoded values.","Ensure the EC2 instance or AWS Lambda function running the application has an IAM role with permissions to access AWS Secrets Manager."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}'::int[],
  'The process begins by storing the sensitive database credentials as a secret in AWS Secrets Manager (Option A). Next, configure automatic rotation for this secret, which typically involves setting a rotation frequency and linking a Lambda function that performs the actual credential update in the database (Option B). Before the application can retrieve the secret, the compute resource (e.g., EC2 instance, Lambda function) running the application must be granted an IAM role with permissions to access Secrets Manager (Option D). Finally, the application code is updated to dynamically retrieve the credentials from Secrets Manager at runtime, ensuring it always uses the current, rotated secret (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_124',
  'A mobile application requires user authentication and management. Order the steps to configure Amazon Cognito User Pools for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to the Amazon Cognito console and create a new User Pool.","Configure user sign-up and sign-in options, including attributes, MFA, and password policies.","Create an App Client within the User Pool to allow your application to interact with it.","Integrate the Amazon Cognito User Pool SDK into your mobile application for user authentication flows."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'To set up Amazon Cognito User Pools, first, navigate to the Cognito console and initiate the creation of a new User Pool (Option A). Next, configure the various settings related to user sign-up and sign-in, such as required attributes, multi-factor authentication, and password policies (Option B). An App Client must then be created within the User Pool; this client provides the necessary credentials for your application to interact with the User Pool (Option C). Finally, integrate the Amazon Cognito User Pool SDK into your mobile application to handle user registration, login, and session management (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_125',
  'An administrator wants to restrict SSH access to an Amazon EC2 instance to only specific IP addresses. Order the steps to achieve this using a Security Group.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to the EC2 dashboard in the AWS Management Console.","Select the Security Group associated with the EC2 instance.","Edit the inbound rules for the Security Group.","Add an inbound rule for SSH (port 22) and specify the trusted IP address range (e.g., your_office_ip/32) as the source."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'To restrict SSH access, first, go to the EC2 dashboard (Option A). Then, identify and select the Security Group that is currently associated with the EC2 instance (Option B). Next, you need to modify the inbound rules of this Security Group (Option C). Finally, add a new inbound rule specifically for SSH (port 22) and, critically, set the source to only the trusted IP address range, rather than 0.0.0.0/0, to limit exposure (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_126',
  'A network engineer needs to configure a Network ACL (NACL) to explicitly deny all inbound traffic from a specific malicious IP address range to a subnet. Order the steps to implement this.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to the VPC dashboard in the AWS Management Console.","Select ''Network ACLs'' and click ''Create network ACL''.","Add an inbound rule with a lower rule number (higher precedence) to explicitly DENY traffic from the malicious IP address range (e.g., 192.0.2.0/24) on all ports.","Associate the new Network ACL with the target subnet."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'To configure a Network ACL, start by navigating to the VPC dashboard (Option A). Then, create a new Network ACL (Option B). The key step is to add an inbound rule with a low rule number (which gives it high precedence) to explicitly DENY traffic from the specified malicious IP range (Option C). Unlike Security Groups, NACLs support explicit deny rules. Finally, associate this new Network ACL with the subnet that needs protection (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_127',
  'A company wants to establish a private, secure connection from their on-premises network to a custom service hosted within their Amazon VPC, without exposing the service to the public internet. Order the steps to set up AWS PrivateLink for this custom service.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a Network Load Balancer (NLB) in your VPC and register your service''s instances as targets.","Create a VPC Endpoint Service configuration, pointing to the NLB.","Grant permissions to the consumer VPC account(s) to connect to your VPC Endpoint Service.","The consumer account creates a VPC Endpoint (Interface Endpoint) to connect to your VPC Endpoint Service."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'To set up AWS PrivateLink for a custom service, first, the service must be fronted by a Network Load Balancer (NLB) within your VPC, with your service instances registered as targets (Option A). Next, a VPC Endpoint Service configuration is created, which references this NLB (Option B). You then explicitly grant permissions to the AWS account(s) of the service consumers, allowing them to create endpoints to your service (Option C). Finally, the consumer account(s) create a VPC Endpoint (specifically an Interface Endpoint) in their own VPC, which establishes a private connection to your VPC Endpoint Service (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

